WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.994 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.994 --> 00:00:04.473 align:middle line:90%
[CREAKING, CLICKING]

00:00:04.473 --> 00:00:10.934 align:middle line:90%


00:00:10.934 --> 00:00:13.540 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: All
right, so welcome to day

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:16.840 align:middle line:90%
two of morphology.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.730 align:middle line:84%
Last time, I was
trying to get you

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:21.303 align:middle line:90%
to believe that it's worth--

00:00:21.303 --> 00:00:22.720 align:middle line:84%
for trying to think
about what you

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.200 align:middle line:84%
have in your mind, the
representations you have

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.685 align:middle line:84%
in your mind of what you can
do with your native language--

00:00:29.685 --> 00:00:31.270 align:middle line:84%
if we're trying to
think about that,

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:35.290 align:middle line:84%
that it's useful to think,
yeah, your mental list

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:38.860 align:middle line:84%
of elements of your basic
elements of your language

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:41.950 align:middle line:84%
consists, maybe, of
a list of morphemes,

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:46.000 align:middle line:84%
where morphemes are these
units that can combine

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.970 align:middle line:90%
in various ways, as we've seen.

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:51.850 align:middle line:84%
Each morpheme we're going to
need to tag with information

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:58.820 align:middle line:84%
about its sound, how it's
pronounced, its meaning,

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:02.530 align:middle line:84%
and then information like
is it a bound morpheme

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:03.670 align:middle line:90%
or a free morpheme?

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:05.560 align:middle line:84%
That is, is it the
kind of morpheme

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:09.290 align:middle line:84%
that needs to combine with
another morpheme or not?

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.580 align:middle line:84%
So there are free
morphemes like "cat,"

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:15.910 align:middle line:84%
where, when we're
listing its sound,

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:18.460 align:middle line:84%
will represent the fact
that it's pronounced "cat,"

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:20.350 align:middle line:84%
and when we're
listing its meaning,

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:22.300 align:middle line:84%
will represent the
fact that it refers

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.040 align:middle line:84%
to a certain type
of small mammal.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:27.310 align:middle line:84%
And when we're listing
whether it's bound or free,

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:29.980 align:middle line:84%
we'll say that it's free
because "cat" is a word.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:31.570 align:middle line:90%
You can say it by itself.

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:35.560 align:middle line:84%
As opposed to the S at the
end of a word like "cats,"

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:38.800 align:middle line:84%
we'll say that's a bound
morpheme because it's not

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:43.170 align:middle line:90%
a word on its own.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.780 align:middle line:84%
And if we're talking
about bound morphemes,

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.872 align:middle line:84%
we ran through bound
morphemes from a bunch

00:01:48.872 --> 00:01:49.830 align:middle line:90%
of different languages.

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:52.290 align:middle line:84%
We saw there are prefixes out
there, there are suffixes,

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.930 align:middle line:84%
and there are other things
we talked about-- infixes,

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:56.188 align:middle line:90%
various other kinds of things.

00:01:56.188 --> 00:01:58.230 align:middle line:84%
There are other kinds of
morphemes to talk about.

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:00.340 align:middle line:90%
We eventually will.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.287 align:middle line:84%
But first-- so what I
said here was first,

00:02:02.287 --> 00:02:04.620 align:middle line:84%
another kind of information
we're going to have to list.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.703 align:middle line:84%
But actually, before that,
is any of that unclear?

00:02:06.703 --> 00:02:08.800 align:middle line:90%
Does that all make sense?

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.975 align:middle line:84%
Is everyone convinced of
the existence of morphemes?

00:02:11.975 --> 00:02:14.100 align:middle line:84%
Are you willing to at least
assume that they exist?

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.780 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.840 align:middle line:84%
So you're used to looking
words up in the dictionary,

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.370 align:middle line:84%
but in your mental
dictionary, maybe

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.162 align:middle line:84%
what you have are not
words but morphemes--

00:02:22.162 --> 00:02:26.200 align:middle line:84%
things that are at least
sometimes smaller than words.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:30.350 align:middle line:84%
So another kind of information
we're going to have to list.

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.890 align:middle line:84%
Think about the
bound morpheme "-al"

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:35.740 align:middle line:84%
that shows up at the end
of industrial, or national,

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:38.560 align:middle line:90%
or autumnal.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.270 align:middle line:84%
That's limited in
where it can go.

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:45.490 align:middle line:84%
So there aren't words like
"assert-al" or "impress-al,"

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:48.705 align:middle line:90%
or "industrializ-al," yeah?

00:02:48.705 --> 00:02:50.080 align:middle line:84%
Again, let me
pause and make sure

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.082 align:middle line:84%
that I'm not saying things
that are just false.

00:02:52.082 --> 00:02:54.040 align:middle line:84%
I think I told you there's
a danger when you're

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.748 align:middle line:84%
a linguist you gradually
get out of touch

00:02:55.748 --> 00:02:56.920 align:middle line:90%
with your native language.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:00.790 align:middle line:84%
But I'm pretty
sure this is true.

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:02.680 align:middle line:84%
Is there a generalization
about the kinds

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:06.460 align:middle line:90%
of things you can add -al to?

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:08.090 align:middle line:90%
What are they?

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:08.590 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:09.230 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Nouns.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:10.438 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Nouns, Yeah.

00:03:10.438 --> 00:03:12.310 align:middle line:84%
So "industry" and
"nation" and "autumn"

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:15.280 align:middle line:84%
are all nouns, as opposed
to "assert," and "impress,"

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.890 align:middle line:84%
and "industrialize,"
which are not nouns.

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:18.843 align:middle line:90%
What are they?

00:03:18.843 --> 00:03:19.550 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Verbs.

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:21.410 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Verbs, Yep.

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:23.690 align:middle line:84%
And if anybody is listening
to this and thinking,

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:24.470 align:middle line:90%
nouns, verbs?

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.640 align:middle line:90%
What the heck?

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:26.360 align:middle line:90%
Don't worry.

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:28.820 align:middle line:84%
We will talk more
about this eventually.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:38.490 align:middle line:84%
Yep, OK, so we just said
there is a word "industrial"

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:39.600 align:middle line:90%
because "-al"--

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.430 align:middle line:84%
maybe I should write
this on the board.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:44.940 align:middle line:84%
Or I would if I thought there
was chalk anywhere, anywhere

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:45.540 align:middle line:90%
at all.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.880 align:middle line:84%
Here we go, I'll write it
on the board right here.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:50.550 align:middle line:90%
That will be helpful.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:51.300 align:middle line:90%
No, I won't.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:53.400 align:middle line:90%
I'll put it over here.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:56.370 align:middle line:84%
We've just said "-al" attaches
to nouns and can't attach

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:57.220 align:middle line:90%
to verbs.

00:03:57.220 --> 00:04:00.520 align:middle line:84%
That's what's going on in
the first of the list there.

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:04.400 align:middle line:84%
So we've got a noun
can have "-al" on it.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:05.985 align:middle line:84%
I'll write that
again over there.

00:04:05.985 --> 00:04:08.730 align:middle line:90%


00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:10.440 align:middle line:84%
This is surely not
the most efficient way

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.420 align:middle line:90%
to use the blackboard.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:13.470 align:middle line:90%
Is it necessary?

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:14.280 align:middle line:90%
Does it work?

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:17.279 align:middle line:84%
Can everybody see one or another
of the things that I wrote?

00:04:17.279 --> 00:04:19.410 align:middle line:84%
Is there anybody who can
only see one of the things

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:20.779 align:middle line:90%
that I wrote?

00:04:20.779 --> 00:04:23.450 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, OK, so it's worth
it to write it twice.

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:26.880 align:middle line:84%
OK, so "-al" attaches to nouns
and doesn't attach to verbs.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:28.520 align:middle line:84%
That's why you can
say "industrial."

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:31.850 align:middle line:84%
It's why you can't say
"industrializ-al," yeah?

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:36.230 align:middle line:84%
Why can you say,
"industrializational"?

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:37.700 align:middle line:90%
That's a word.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:39.590 align:middle line:90%
It's kind of a long word.

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:42.170 align:middle line:84%
"Industrializational,"
I guess, means having

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:45.110 align:middle line:90%
to do with industrialization.

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:45.830 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:47.570 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because
"industrialization" is a noun?

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:49.528 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
"Industrialization" is a noun.

00:04:49.528 --> 00:04:51.920 align:middle line:84%
So "-ation" is attaching
to "industrialize,"

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:55.110 align:middle line:84%
and it is apparently
creating a noun.

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:57.320 align:middle line:84%
So at least for some
kinds of morphemes,

00:04:57.320 --> 00:05:00.410 align:middle line:84%
we're going to want to list the
kinds of things they attach to,

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:02.790 align:middle line:84%
their input, and
then their output.

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:06.110 align:middle line:84%
So "-ation" can attach to a
verb like "industrialize,"

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:08.270 align:middle line:90%
and it can create a noun.

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:14.030 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so "-ation"
attaches to a verb,

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:16.670 align:middle line:90%
and then what you get is a noun.

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:23.270 align:middle line:84%
Say that again, if you take
a verb and add "-ation,"

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:25.550 align:middle line:90%
what you end up with is a noun.

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:27.525 align:middle line:90%
And that sort of--

00:05:27.525 --> 00:05:29.900 align:middle line:84%
we have intuitions about which
things are nouns and which

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:30.483 align:middle line:90%
things aren't.

00:05:30.483 --> 00:05:33.920 align:middle line:84%
We have the intuition that
"industrialization" is a noun,

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:37.290 align:middle line:84%
but now we can check our
intuition by asking ourselves,

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:39.560 align:middle line:84%
well, we know that
"-al" attaches to nouns.

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:41.540 align:middle line:84%
Can we attach "-al" to
"industrialization"?

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:42.180 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:47.280 align:middle line:84%
Yes, our intuition is
right; that's a noun.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:50.660 align:middle line:84%
OK, cool, so there are at least
some kinds of morphemes which

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:54.350 align:middle line:84%
convert words into
other types of words,

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:59.570 align:middle line:84%
convert nouns into verbs
or, in the case of "-al,"

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:02.300 align:middle line:90%
verbs into adjectives--

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.520 align:middle line:90%
sorry, nouns into adjectives.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:11.090 align:middle line:84%
OK, so adding some things to
the list I showed you before,

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:14.853 align:middle line:84%
the lexicon has morphemes in
it, and morphemes all contain

00:06:14.853 --> 00:06:16.520 align:middle line:84%
the information about
sound and meaning,

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:18.890 align:middle line:84%
and whether it's bound or
free, and if it's bound,

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:21.320 align:middle line:84%
what kind of bound morpheme
it is-- a prefix, or a suffix,

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:26.070 align:middle line:84%
or a tone, or an
infix, or whatever--

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:29.420 align:middle line:84%
and also what kind of
morpheme they can attach to.

00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:31.580 align:middle line:84%
Do they attach to nouns,
or verbs, or what?

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:36.620 align:middle line:84%
And, at least for some kinds of
morphemes, what the result is--

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:39.090 align:middle line:84%
whether you create a noun,
or a verb, or an adjective,

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:39.590 align:middle line:90%
or whatever.

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:41.510 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, they both sound OK.

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:45.540 align:middle line:90%


00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:48.970 align:middle line:84%
Actually, sometimes, we'll
have to say more than that.

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:52.920 align:middle line:84%
So "sincere," "chaste,"
"scarce," "curious,"

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:55.110 align:middle line:90%
"deep," "wide," and "warm"--

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:57.920 align:middle line:90%
what are those?

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:00.230 align:middle line:90%
They're adjectives.

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:04.910 align:middle line:84%
And when you add "-ity" to
"sincere," you get "sincerity."

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:07.730 align:middle line:84%
When you add "-ity" to
"chaste," you get "chastity."

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:10.956 align:middle line:84%
And what are "sincerity"
and "chastity"?

00:07:10.956 --> 00:07:12.238 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: They're nouns.

00:07:12.238 --> 00:07:13.530 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: They're nouns.

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:16.080 align:middle line:84%
So "-ity" is attaching
to an adjective,

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:18.300 align:middle line:90%
and it's making a noun.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:22.570 align:middle line:84%
When you add "-th" to
"deep," you get "depth."

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:25.870 align:middle line:84%
Now, when you add "-th" to
"wide," you get "width."

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:28.000 align:middle line:84%
So "-th" is attaching
to an adjective,

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:29.140 align:middle line:90%
and what is it creating?

00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:30.450 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Noun.

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:32.610 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: A noun, yeah.

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:34.530 align:middle line:84%
So here we have
two suffixes that

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:35.950 align:middle line:90%
both seem to do the same thing.

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:37.950 align:middle line:84%
They both attach to
adjectives and create nouns.

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:41.340 align:middle line:90%


00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:43.680 align:middle line:84%
But we're going to have
to say something else,

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:49.770 align:middle line:84%
because you cannot say
"deepity," or "sincereth."

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:51.630 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, those are not words.

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:55.020 align:middle line:84%
So you can't add "-th" to
the adjectives at the top,

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:58.590 align:middle line:84%
and you can't add "-ity" to
the adjectives at the bottom.

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:00.210 align:middle line:84%
So sometimes it's
not enough to say,

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:01.620 align:middle line:90%
this attaches to adjectives.

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:04.855 align:middle line:84%
You have to say, it attaches
to adjectives in this list,

00:08:04.855 --> 00:08:06.480 align:middle line:84%
or we have to understand
something more

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:08.167 align:middle line:90%
about the adjectives.

00:08:08.167 --> 00:08:10.000 align:middle line:84%
Anybody have a guess
about why there are two

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:13.010 align:middle line:90%
kinds of adjectives in English?

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:13.670 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:08:13.670 --> 00:08:16.045 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was going to say
that some are Latin-derived,

00:08:16.045 --> 00:08:18.290 align:middle line:84%
and others are just from
Old English or Germanic.

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:19.400 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I think
you were going to be right,

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:21.645 align:middle line:84%
but did you convince
yourself not to say that?

00:08:21.645 --> 00:08:24.020 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because "scarce"
does not come from Latin, so--

00:08:24.020 --> 00:08:26.180 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah,
but it should have, yeah.

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:29.650 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so the ones at the
top, yes, look Latinate,

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:30.650 align:middle line:90%
you're absolutely right.

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:34.010 align:middle line:84%
And the ones at the
bottom look Germanic.

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:35.150 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's right.

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:38.570 align:middle line:84%
So there are a few things
like this where it's

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:40.159 align:middle line:90%
a result of English history--

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:42.459 align:middle line:84%
that we have suffixes
that we got from-- "-ity"

00:08:42.459 --> 00:08:46.145 align:middle line:84%
is something that we got
from French and "-th"

00:08:46.145 --> 00:08:48.770 align:middle line:84%
is something we
got from Germanic.

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:53.067 align:middle line:84%
And so yeah, these suffixes
are maintaining their history

00:08:53.067 --> 00:08:53.900 align:middle line:90%
to a certain extent.

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:54.400 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:54.477 align:middle line:90%


00:08:54.477 --> 00:08:57.060 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: "-ity," I don't know
if this is a real thing or just

00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:59.450 align:middle line:84%
me thinking, but it reminds
me of Spanish "-idad."

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:02.870 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: It is related
to that, yes, Yeah, score.

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:05.390 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, good point, yep.

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:09.290 align:middle line:84%
OK, so I mean, you can be
a native speaker of English

00:09:09.290 --> 00:09:10.590 align:middle line:90%
and not know this.

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:13.610 align:middle line:84%
It's not like we are all
remembering, oh, yeah,

00:09:13.610 --> 00:09:15.860 align:middle line:84%
those are the
Germanic adjectives,

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:17.840 align:middle line:84%
those are the
Latinate adjectives.

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:21.590 align:middle line:84%
Be sure to add "-ity" to that
one and "-th" to that one.

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:23.450 align:middle line:84%
But on some level,
we have to say, yeah,

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:25.670 align:middle line:84%
there are adjectives of
class 1 and adjectives

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:26.960 align:middle line:90%
of class 2 or something.

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:29.915 align:middle line:84%
There are purple adjectives and
green adjectives or something.

00:09:29.915 --> 00:09:31.790 align:middle line:84%
We have to distinguish
these from each other.

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:34.832 align:middle line:90%


00:09:34.832 --> 00:09:36.540 align:middle line:84%
I'm giving you English
examples, but this

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:37.665 align:middle line:90%
is a very common situation.

00:09:37.665 --> 00:09:41.690 align:middle line:84%
Lots of languages have been in
intense borrowing situations

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:46.930 align:middle line:84%
at some point in their history
with weird results like this.

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:53.720 align:middle line:84%
OK, now we've talked
about this a little bit.

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:57.790 align:middle line:84%
So I said when you add the
plural suffix to "cats"

00:09:57.790 --> 00:10:00.850 align:middle line:84%
and "dogs," one of the
first things I said to you,

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:03.790 align:middle line:84%
it's pronounced differently on
"cats" than it is on "dogs."

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:07.570 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, and we talked about
why, and we're going

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:09.820 align:middle line:90%
to talk more about why later.

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:11.890 align:middle line:84%
This is the kind of thing
that happens sometimes

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:14.170 align:middle line:84%
when you add one morpheme
to another morpheme--

00:10:14.170 --> 00:10:17.650 align:middle line:84%
one or another or both of the
morphemes changes a little bit.

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:19.240 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk a lot
about this as we

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:23.320 align:middle line:84%
start talking about
sound change, phonology,

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:25.250 align:middle line:90%
which is our next topic.

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:29.710 align:middle line:84%
But for now, we can just pause
and notice, if you add "-al"

00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:31.870 align:middle line:84%
to "electric," you
get "electrical."

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:34.990 align:middle line:84%
But if you add
"-ity" to "electric,"

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:37.780 align:middle line:90%
you don't get "elec-trick-ity".

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:39.315 align:middle line:90%
You get "electricity."

00:10:39.315 --> 00:10:40.690 align:middle line:84%
It's not because
"elec-trick-ity"

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:41.890 align:middle line:90%
would be hard to say.

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:45.890 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, it's just we have this
idiosyncratic sound change--

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:48.250 align:middle line:84%
which we inherited,
again, from French--

00:10:48.250 --> 00:10:50.060 align:middle line:84%
that softens the
K to an S there.

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:50.560 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:52.000 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
idiosyncratic?

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:52.870 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I'm
sorry, say it again?

00:10:52.870 --> 00:10:54.578 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What does
"idiosyncratic" mean?

00:10:54.578 --> 00:10:57.760 align:middle line:84%
Oh, "idiosyncratic"
means-- what does it mean?

00:10:57.760 --> 00:10:59.510 align:middle line:84%
What do I mean when I
say "idiosyncratic?"

00:10:59.510 --> 00:11:01.720 align:middle line:84%
It means you
sometimes have to say,

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:04.420 align:middle line:84%
this particular morpheme
has two different forms

00:11:04.420 --> 00:11:06.290 align:middle line:84%
depending on what
it combines with.

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:07.850 align:middle line:84%
So you have to say
more about that.

00:11:07.850 --> 00:11:10.060 align:middle line:84%
So "idiosyncratic" means
special, having something

00:11:10.060 --> 00:11:11.830 align:middle line:90%
special to do with that.

00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:15.880 align:middle line:84%
So the suffix "-ic" at
the end of "electric"

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:19.000 align:middle line:84%
will change to
"-is" before "-ity."

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:21.160 align:middle line:84%
And there's more
to say than that,

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:26.380 align:middle line:84%
but you have to say something
that has that consequence.

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:31.360 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, the past tense, it's
like the plural suffix, which

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:35.080 align:middle line:84%
can be either "z" or "s,"
like in "cats" and "dogs,"

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:37.190 align:middle line:90%
has a bunch of different forms.

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:40.150 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, it can be more of
a "d" sound like in "hummed."

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:42.730 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes it can be more of
a "t" sound like in "leaped"

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:47.290 align:middle line:84%
[pronounced like "leapt"] And
the verb itself can undergo

00:11:47.290 --> 00:11:49.180 align:middle line:84%
changes when you add
the suffix to it.

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:51.340 align:middle line:84%
So the past tense of
"hum" is "hummed."

00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:53.047 align:middle line:90%
That's all fairly peaceful.

00:11:53.047 --> 00:11:55.630 align:middle line:84%
But the past tense of "leap" is
not "leaped" [with "d" sound],

00:11:55.630 --> 00:11:58.270 align:middle line:90%
it's "leaped" [with "t" sound].

00:11:58.270 --> 00:12:01.900 align:middle line:84%
The past tense "go" is
not "goed," it's "went."

00:12:01.900 --> 00:12:03.730 align:middle line:90%
This is a case where we--

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:05.290 align:middle line:90%
it's what's called suppletion.

00:12:05.290 --> 00:12:08.500 align:middle line:84%
So English speakers
at some point

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:10.810 align:middle line:84%
in the history of English,
for mysterious reasons

00:12:10.810 --> 00:12:13.540 align:middle line:84%
of their own, decided that
the past tense of "go"

00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:16.330 align:middle line:84%
should borrow-- that "go"
should borrow its past tense

00:12:16.330 --> 00:12:18.170 align:middle line:90%
from another verb.

00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:20.050 align:middle line:84%
So for the past
tense of "go," we

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:21.760 align:middle line:84%
use the past tense
of another verb,

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:24.800 align:middle line:84%
"wend," as in "to
wend your way."

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:27.640 align:middle line:84%
And so the result of the past
tense go, it's no longer "go."

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:30.760 align:middle line:90%
It Is now "went."

00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:32.230 align:middle line:90%
Languages do things like this.

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:34.870 align:middle line:84%
It'd be interesting to
try to understand why.

00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:37.690 align:middle line:84%
Or similarly, "sing" and
"sang," past tense of "sing,"

00:12:37.690 --> 00:12:39.697 align:middle line:84%
there's no "d" or
"t" or anything.

00:12:39.697 --> 00:12:40.780 align:middle line:90%
You just change the vowel.

00:12:40.780 --> 00:12:44.110 align:middle line:84%
So there are cases
where a morpheme

00:12:44.110 --> 00:12:45.790 align:middle line:84%
will have different
forms depending

00:12:45.790 --> 00:12:47.230 align:middle line:90%
on what it's combining with.

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:49.570 align:middle line:84%
That's the quick and dirty
way of describing everything

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:52.650 align:middle line:90%
that's on the screen here.

00:12:52.650 --> 00:12:54.520 align:middle line:84%
The technical term for
what's going on here,

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:56.228 align:middle line:84%
we say that when you
have a morpheme that

00:12:56.228 --> 00:12:58.810 align:middle line:84%
has two different forms,
the two different forms--

00:12:58.810 --> 00:13:02.080 align:middle line:84%
or multiple different forms,
sometimes it's more than two--

00:13:02.080 --> 00:13:03.610 align:middle line:84%
the different
forms of a morpheme

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:06.610 align:middle line:84%
that it takes under different
morphological circumstances,

00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:08.680 align:middle line:84%
those things are
called allomorphs.

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:14.590 align:middle line:84%
So we say that, for
example, the "d" and the "t"

00:13:14.590 --> 00:13:18.220 align:middle line:84%
past tenses are two
allomorphs of the past tense,

00:13:18.220 --> 00:13:20.680 align:middle line:84%
or that "leap" and "leap"
[pronounced like "lep"] are

00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:24.070 align:middle line:84%
the elements of the verb "leap,"
the word that means "jump."

00:13:24.070 --> 00:13:25.780 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:13:25.780 --> 00:13:29.350 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: For example,
when we [INAUDIBLE]..

00:13:29.350 --> 00:13:30.850 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Oh, I'm just trying

00:13:30.850 --> 00:13:32.267 align:middle line:84%
to call your
attention to the fact

00:13:32.267 --> 00:13:33.760 align:middle line:84%
that it's pronounced
differently.

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:36.250 align:middle line:84%
Eventually, those brackets
will mean something,

00:13:36.250 --> 00:13:37.990 align:middle line:84%
but we haven't
gotten far enough yet

00:13:37.990 --> 00:13:40.000 align:middle line:90%
to get them to mean anything.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:42.940 align:middle line:84%
So right now, all I'm doing is
calling-- if I had said "leap,

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:44.465 align:middle line:84%
leaped," I would
have spelled "leap"

00:13:44.465 --> 00:13:46.840 align:middle line:84%
[pronounced like "lep"] the
same way I'm spelling "leap."

00:13:46.840 --> 00:13:48.423 align:middle line:84%
We would just pronounce
it differently

00:13:48.423 --> 00:13:50.960 align:middle line:84%
because English
spelling is insane.

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:53.590 align:middle line:84%
And so I'm just putting
the brackets there

00:13:53.590 --> 00:13:55.000 align:middle line:90%
as a warning sign--

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:59.110 align:middle line:84%
here's a place where the
spelling is unreliable.

00:13:59.110 --> 00:13:59.733 align:middle line:90%
Good question.

00:13:59.733 --> 00:14:01.900 align:middle line:84%
Eventually, we'll figure
out how to talk about that.

00:14:01.900 --> 00:14:03.016 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:14:03.016 --> 00:14:04.474 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:14:04.474 --> 00:14:08.213 align:middle line:90%


00:14:08.213 --> 00:14:09.130 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:14:09.130 --> 00:14:12.341 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So does that
define [INAUDIBLE]..

00:14:12.341 --> 00:14:16.190 align:middle line:90%


00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:20.111 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, so right,
so I called it "d" and "t"

00:14:20.111 --> 00:14:24.080 align:middle line:84%
because "hummed," the past tense
of "hum," although we spell it

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:27.620 align:middle line:84%
with an "e" before
the "d," you say it,

00:14:27.620 --> 00:14:29.510 align:middle line:84%
I think there's only
one vowel in that word.

00:14:29.510 --> 00:14:31.100 align:middle line:90%
It's "hummed," right?

00:14:31.100 --> 00:14:33.110 align:middle line:90%
We're not saying "humm-ed."

00:14:33.110 --> 00:14:34.812 align:middle line:84%
That's not the past
tense of "hummed."

00:14:34.812 --> 00:14:35.762 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:35.762 --> 00:14:37.220 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, yeah, so I'm

00:14:37.220 --> 00:14:40.370 align:middle line:84%
trying to just spell out
sound, which is hard because we

00:14:40.370 --> 00:14:41.780 align:middle line:90%
haven't done phonology yet.

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:45.270 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, but that's a
really good question.

00:14:45.270 --> 00:14:48.230 align:middle line:84%
This is one of many places where
English spelling will get you

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:51.380 align:middle line:84%
in trouble because
English is spelled

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:54.540 align:middle line:84%
according to rules that are
designed to repel invaders,

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:55.040 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:57.720 align:middle line:90%


00:14:57.720 --> 00:14:58.370 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:14:58.370 --> 00:14:59.750 align:middle line:90%
Any questions about any of this?

00:14:59.750 --> 00:15:02.210 align:middle line:84%
These are really good
questions you guys are asking.

00:15:02.210 --> 00:15:03.830 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:15:03.830 --> 00:15:04.330 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:15:04.330 --> 00:15:07.680 align:middle line:90%


00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:14.920 align:middle line:90%
OK, yeah.

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:21.380 align:middle line:84%
So sometimes-- so we will get a
chance to talk more-- in fact,

00:15:21.380 --> 00:15:24.340 align:middle line:84%
we'll talk more very
soon- about how exactly

00:15:24.340 --> 00:15:27.670 align:middle line:84%
to talk about allomorphy,
this property of morphemes

00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:31.030 align:middle line:84%
of having more than one
form, of having allomorphs.

00:15:31.030 --> 00:15:33.430 align:middle line:84%
All we've said so
far is, sometimes

00:15:33.430 --> 00:15:34.940 align:middle line:90%
morphemes have allomorphs.

00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:36.790 align:middle line:84%
So depending on what
they combine with,

00:15:36.790 --> 00:15:39.980 align:middle line:90%
they take different forms.

00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:42.460 align:middle line:84%
There's often what's
called a default allomorph.

00:15:42.460 --> 00:15:47.710 align:middle line:84%
That is, there's a morpheme
that is the basic one that

00:15:47.710 --> 00:15:49.267 align:middle line:90%
shows up most of the time.

00:15:49.267 --> 00:15:51.100 align:middle line:84%
And when we're making
our special statements

00:15:51.100 --> 00:15:53.320 align:middle line:84%
about other forms,
well, they're going

00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:54.542 align:middle line:90%
to be about the other forms.

00:15:54.542 --> 00:15:56.500 align:middle line:84%
So there's going to be
the form of the morpheme

00:15:56.500 --> 00:15:57.460 align:middle line:90%
that you start off with.

00:15:57.460 --> 00:15:58.877 align:middle line:84%
And then, you will
do things to it

00:15:58.877 --> 00:16:01.610 align:middle line:90%
to create the other allomorphs.

00:16:01.610 --> 00:16:06.220 align:middle line:84%
So for example, the
default assumption

00:16:06.220 --> 00:16:08.720 align:middle line:84%
if I'm telling you about a
verb, I've made up a verb.

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:10.180 align:middle line:90%
It's "fleep," "to fleep."

00:16:10.180 --> 00:16:14.950 align:middle line:84%
It's a special move in soccer
in which you do a headstand

00:16:14.950 --> 00:16:17.380 align:middle line:84%
and kick with your
feet over your head.

00:16:17.380 --> 00:16:18.280 align:middle line:90%
I just made that up.

00:16:18.280 --> 00:16:20.140 align:middle line:84%
The past tense of
"fleep," you're

00:16:20.140 --> 00:16:21.970 align:middle line:84%
going to assume
that it's "fleeped."

00:16:21.970 --> 00:16:24.790 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, it could be "flept."

00:16:24.790 --> 00:16:27.162 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, it could be like leap.

00:16:27.162 --> 00:16:29.120 align:middle line:84%
But you're going to assume
that it's "fleeped."

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:32.530 align:middle line:84%
That is the default. You
assume that morphemes

00:16:32.530 --> 00:16:36.250 align:middle line:84%
don't have allomorphs other
than the one, in this case, that

00:16:36.250 --> 00:16:39.960 align:middle line:84%
shows up in ordinary present
tense circumstances when

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:40.980 align:middle line:90%
they're not combining.

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:43.620 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:45.390 align:middle line:84%
Is this any of this
surprising or weird?

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:45.947 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:16:45.947 --> 00:16:47.530 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Does that
particular example

00:16:47.530 --> 00:16:48.905 align:middle line:84%
have anything to
do with the fact

00:16:48.905 --> 00:16:50.367 align:middle line:84%
that it looks like
the word "leap"?

00:16:50.367 --> 00:16:51.950 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I
made it up that way

00:16:51.950 --> 00:16:54.765 align:middle line:90%
to make you think that.

00:16:54.765 --> 00:16:57.140 align:middle line:84%
So she asked, does that have
anything to do with the fact

00:16:57.140 --> 00:16:58.850 align:middle line:84%
that it looks like
the verb "leap?"

00:16:58.850 --> 00:17:02.780 align:middle line:84%
I guess the point I was making
is we can't have a general rule

00:17:02.780 --> 00:17:05.780 align:middle line:84%
that if you're making the
past tense of a verb that ends

00:17:05.780 --> 00:17:09.740 align:middle line:84%
in "-eap," that it's
going to change to "-ep."

00:17:09.740 --> 00:17:12.710 align:middle line:84%
When we're trying to figure out
what's going on with "leap,"

00:17:12.710 --> 00:17:14.030 align:middle line:90%
that can't be what's going on.

00:17:14.030 --> 00:17:16.155 align:middle line:84%
I guess I could have made
that point in other ways.

00:17:16.155 --> 00:17:19.220 align:middle line:84%
We have verbs like "heap,"
like to heap up sand,

00:17:19.220 --> 00:17:21.500 align:middle line:84%
and the past tense of
"heap" is not "hept," right?

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:23.450 align:middle line:90%
It's "heaped."

00:17:23.450 --> 00:17:25.460 align:middle line:84%
But similarly, if I
make up a new verb,

00:17:25.460 --> 00:17:27.710 align:middle line:84%
you're not going to
apply what happens

00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:30.380 align:middle line:90%
in "leap" to the new verb.

00:17:30.380 --> 00:17:31.160 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:17:31.160 --> 00:17:33.320 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Are there any
specific characteristics

00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:36.170 align:middle line:84%
that would deter someone from
going towards the default

00:17:36.170 --> 00:17:36.950 align:middle line:90%
allomorph?

00:17:36.950 --> 00:17:39.680 align:middle line:84%
Like if you made
another made-up word,

00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:41.181 align:middle line:84%
would there be any
characteristics

00:17:41.181 --> 00:17:43.723 align:middle line:84%
about that word that would have
us moving towards [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:43.723 --> 00:17:44.735 align:middle line:90%
the "t" at the end?

00:17:44.735 --> 00:17:45.860 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, I see.

00:17:45.860 --> 00:17:49.110 align:middle line:90%


00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:55.140 align:middle line:84%
I don't think there's any way
for me to induce you to do

00:17:55.140 --> 00:17:58.650 align:middle line:84%
the "leap"/"lept" thing other
than by telling you, oh,

00:17:58.650 --> 00:18:02.130 align:middle line:84%
by the way, the past
tense of "leap" is "lept."

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:03.630 align:middle line:84%
But it's interesting
to think about.

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:05.670 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, Raquel, you were
about to comment on that.

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:07.128 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was
thinking like maybe

00:18:07.128 --> 00:18:09.960 align:middle line:84%
if the word, you know it
was in a specific discipline

00:18:09.960 --> 00:18:13.050 align:middle line:84%
where the default one
for that discipline

00:18:13.050 --> 00:18:15.210 align:middle line:84%
is to do something that's
viewed as a regular

00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:17.940 align:middle line:84%
compared to the average thing--
like if you had an "ae" mean

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:20.520 align:middle line:84%
the plural, and you normally
don't do that for all words,

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:22.500 align:middle line:84%
but you might do it
for, I don't know,

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:23.583 align:middle line:90%
weird plants or something.

00:18:23.583 --> 00:18:24.500 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:18:24.500 --> 00:18:26.430 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You might
default to weird plants.

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:26.790 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I see.

00:18:26.790 --> 00:18:27.520 align:middle line:90%
I see what you mean.

00:18:27.520 --> 00:18:28.978 align:middle line:84%
And there are
languages, certainly,

00:18:28.978 --> 00:18:31.350 align:middle line:84%
in which it might be easier
to make a point like that.

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:33.660 align:middle line:90%
I guess the other thing to say--

00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:36.660 align:middle line:84%
the point you're
raising brings this up--

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:39.240 align:middle line:84%
people sometimes claim
anyway that these kinds

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:42.720 align:middle line:84%
of irregular
allomorphy, these things

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:45.480 align:middle line:84%
you just have to memorize
about how things combine,

00:18:45.480 --> 00:18:49.620 align:middle line:84%
they'll go away if
you-- they'll sometimes

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:52.120 align:middle line:84%
go away if you combine a
morpheme with something else.

00:18:52.120 --> 00:18:54.360 align:middle line:84%
So the classic example,
for some reason,

00:18:54.360 --> 00:19:03.274 align:middle line:84%
the plural of "leaf," the
things on trees, is "leaves."

00:19:03.274 --> 00:19:07.285 align:middle line:84%
And here, I'll put
it over here, too.

00:19:07.285 --> 00:19:11.710 align:middle line:90%


00:19:11.710 --> 00:19:14.890 align:middle line:84%
But I've heard it
claimed, anyway,

00:19:14.890 --> 00:19:17.590 align:middle line:84%
that if you're talking
about the athletic team,

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:19.570 align:middle line:90%
the Toronto Maple Leaf--

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:22.030 align:middle line:84%
so the singular would be
the "Toronto Maple Leaf"--

00:19:22.030 --> 00:19:26.500 align:middle line:84%
that the plural is the "Toronto
Maple Leafs," and not the

00:19:26.500 --> 00:19:29.430 align:middle line:90%
"Toronto Maple Leaves."

00:19:29.430 --> 00:19:30.640 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to pause.

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:34.005 align:middle line:84%
I'm not enough of the sports fan
to know whether this is true.

00:19:34.005 --> 00:19:35.520 align:middle line:90%
So I'm getting a thumbs up.

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:39.965 align:middle line:84%
Anybody here from Toronto, or
is anybody a Maple Leafs fan?

00:19:39.965 --> 00:19:41.340 align:middle line:84%
Anyone know whether
this is true?

00:19:41.340 --> 00:19:42.780 align:middle line:84%
Presumably, is the kind of
thing you could find out

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:44.100 align:middle line:90%
by looking at their website--

00:19:44.100 --> 00:19:45.990 align:middle line:84%
what does it say
on their T-shirts?

00:19:45.990 --> 00:19:49.538 align:middle line:84%
But this is a claim
that I've heard, anyway.

00:19:49.538 --> 00:19:52.080 align:middle line:84%
So there are some cases where
this weird allomorphy goes away

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:54.360 align:middle line:84%
because you've made it
part of this compound,

00:19:54.360 --> 00:19:55.930 align:middle line:84%
or you've made it
part of a name.

00:19:55.930 --> 00:19:58.838 align:middle line:90%
So it isn't just about--

00:19:58.838 --> 00:20:00.630 align:middle line:84%
if we're talking about
actual maple leaves,

00:20:00.630 --> 00:20:03.068 align:middle line:84%
like leaves on a
tree, then those

00:20:03.068 --> 00:20:05.610 align:middle line:84%
are definitely "maple leaves,"
and something about them being

00:20:05.610 --> 00:20:08.076 align:middle line:90%
the name of an athletic team.

00:20:08.076 --> 00:20:11.500 align:middle line:84%
And somebody else had a hand
that I ruthlessly ignored.

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:12.000 align:middle line:90%
Oh, Joseph?

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:13.372 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:13.372 --> 00:20:14.580 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, they do?

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:15.717 align:middle line:90%
You checked.

00:20:15.717 --> 00:20:16.800 align:middle line:90%
This is the kind of thing.

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:19.290 align:middle line:84%
[LAUGHS] I can tell
sometimes that I

00:20:19.290 --> 00:20:22.460 align:middle line:84%
was born in a different
century, because there

00:20:22.460 --> 00:20:24.960 align:middle line:84%
are all kinds of things where
I raise interesting questions,

00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:26.700 align:middle line:84%
and then in class,
someone will be like, yes,

00:20:26.700 --> 00:20:27.670 align:middle line:90%
and here is the answer.

00:20:27.670 --> 00:20:31.910 align:middle line:84%
Like, oh, right, yeah, we have
the ability to do that now.

00:20:31.910 --> 00:20:33.110 align:middle line:90%
Anything else?

00:20:33.110 --> 00:20:36.022 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What do you think the
past tense of "slingshot" is?

00:20:36.022 --> 00:20:37.950 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Ooh!

00:20:37.950 --> 00:20:40.817 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So "slingshotted" and
"slingshot" both feel wrong.

00:20:40.817 --> 00:20:41.650 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: OK.

00:20:41.650 --> 00:20:43.730 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:43.730 --> 00:20:46.100 align:middle line:90%
"Slingshoots."

00:20:46.100 --> 00:20:49.010 align:middle line:84%
So question was, what's the
past tense of "slingshot"?

00:20:49.010 --> 00:20:52.433 align:middle line:84%
Two possibilities--
"slingshotted" and "slingshot."

00:20:52.433 --> 00:20:53.850 align:middle line:84%
And there was
another possibility,

00:20:53.850 --> 00:20:56.660 align:middle line:84%
which we will ignore
for reasons of timing.

00:20:56.660 --> 00:21:01.220 align:middle line:84%
Who thinks the past tense of
"slingshot" is "slingshotted"?

00:21:01.220 --> 00:21:03.620 align:middle line:90%
Who thinks it's "slingshot"?

00:21:03.620 --> 00:21:05.340 align:middle line:90%
OK, interesting.

00:21:05.340 --> 00:21:07.220 align:middle line:90%
There are some examples--

00:21:07.220 --> 00:21:08.932 align:middle line:84%
does anybody have
another alternative?

00:21:08.932 --> 00:21:09.890 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Slingshote."

00:21:09.890 --> 00:21:10.610 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "Slingshote"?

00:21:10.610 --> 00:21:11.270 align:middle line:90%
No!

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:13.303 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHS] Shame, for shame.

00:21:13.303 --> 00:21:14.720 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
"Slingshut."

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:18.440 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "Slung,"
"slung," "shoot"-- no.

00:21:18.440 --> 00:21:19.400 align:middle line:90%
Also no.

00:21:19.400 --> 00:21:22.190 align:middle line:84%
There are some examples
of verbs or morphemes

00:21:22.190 --> 00:21:23.420 align:middle line:90%
that if you combine them--

00:21:23.420 --> 00:21:24.350 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:24.350 --> 00:21:26.360 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Sorry,
if you combine them

00:21:26.360 --> 00:21:28.310 align:middle line:84%
with other morphemes
that no one knows,

00:21:28.310 --> 00:21:30.540 align:middle line:84%
this is kind of
like "slingshot."

00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:34.220 align:middle line:90%
So take the verb "to stride."

00:21:34.220 --> 00:21:37.070 align:middle line:90%
So the past tense is "strode."

00:21:37.070 --> 00:21:41.162 align:middle line:84%
And then, the
participle was, he has--

00:21:41.162 --> 00:21:43.763 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] "stride"--

00:21:43.763 --> 00:21:44.930 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "Stridden."

00:21:44.930 --> 00:21:46.610 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:46.610 --> 00:21:48.410 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
"Strode," "strod."

00:21:48.410 --> 00:21:50.930 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHS] Who thinks it's--

00:21:50.930 --> 00:21:52.040 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:52.040 --> 00:21:54.530 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Who
thinks it's "stridden"?

00:21:54.530 --> 00:21:56.750 align:middle line:90%
Who thinks it's "strode"?

00:21:56.750 --> 00:21:58.700 align:middle line:84%
Who has something else
they'd like it to be?

00:21:58.700 --> 00:21:58.970 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Strud."

00:21:58.970 --> 00:22:00.140 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "Strud?"

00:22:00.140 --> 00:22:01.820 align:middle line:90%
Any votes for "strud"?

00:22:01.820 --> 00:22:04.340 align:middle line:90%
No, you're all alone, I'm sorry.

00:22:04.340 --> 00:22:06.800 align:middle line:84%
So this is a classic
example of a word where

00:22:06.800 --> 00:22:09.650 align:middle line:84%
English speakers just
are not sure what

00:22:09.650 --> 00:22:10.820 align:middle line:90%
the participle of this is.

00:22:10.820 --> 00:22:11.450 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Smite" as well.

00:22:11.450 --> 00:22:12.530 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Sorry?

00:22:12.530 --> 00:22:14.655 align:middle line:84%
"Smite" is another
good example, yes.

00:22:14.655 --> 00:22:15.710 align:middle line:90%
So that one--

00:22:15.710 --> 00:22:17.758 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:22:17.758 --> 00:22:19.550 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: That
one, yeah, so for me,

00:22:19.550 --> 00:22:23.990 align:middle line:84%
so "smitten" has this
other use, right?

00:22:23.990 --> 00:22:26.460 align:middle line:84%
You can be "smitten"
with someone--

00:22:26.460 --> 00:22:28.520 align:middle line:90%
which I think is interfering.

00:22:28.520 --> 00:22:34.358 align:middle line:84%
Yep, OK, so lots of fun things
to think about allomorphy.

00:22:34.358 --> 00:22:37.070 align:middle line:84%
OK, so I'm going through
all this partly--

00:22:37.070 --> 00:22:40.640 align:middle line:84%
so I want to go back to the
stuff about morphemes combining

00:22:40.640 --> 00:22:45.320 align:middle line:84%
with things and creating other
things because so far, we

00:22:45.320 --> 00:22:48.530 align:middle line:84%
have talked about various
cases where morphemes combined

00:22:48.530 --> 00:22:50.038 align:middle line:84%
with other morphemes,
and we've also

00:22:50.038 --> 00:22:51.830 align:middle line:84%
talked about cases
where morphemes combined

00:22:51.830 --> 00:22:53.000 align:middle line:90%
with more than one morpheme.

00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:55.340 align:middle line:84%
And I kind want to talk
about that a little bit

00:22:55.340 --> 00:22:57.140 align:middle line:84%
carefully, because
it's going to be

00:22:57.140 --> 00:22:59.390 align:middle line:84%
something that's
going to be useful

00:22:59.390 --> 00:23:01.212 align:middle line:90%
as we go forward in the class.

00:23:01.212 --> 00:23:03.170 align:middle line:84%
So let's talk about these
particular morphemes.

00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:05.720 align:middle line:90%
Think about the suffix "-ment."

00:23:05.720 --> 00:23:09.350 align:middle line:84%
What does "-ment" attach
to, and what does it create?

00:23:09.350 --> 00:23:11.990 align:middle line:84%
So you can say things like
"government" and "treatment."

00:23:11.990 --> 00:23:15.840 align:middle line:84%
You cannot say things like
"bodyment" or "powerment."

00:23:15.840 --> 00:23:19.690 align:middle line:90%
What does "-ment" attach to?

00:23:19.690 --> 00:23:22.120 align:middle line:90%
What are "govern" and "treat"?

00:23:22.120 --> 00:23:22.840 align:middle line:90%
Verbs.

00:23:22.840 --> 00:23:24.010 align:middle line:90%
Verbs, OK.

00:23:24.010 --> 00:23:29.000 align:middle line:84%
So "-ment"-- erase
some stuff here--

00:23:29.000 --> 00:23:36.214 align:middle line:84%
put "-ment," it attaches to
a verb, and it creates what?

00:23:36.214 --> 00:23:36.960 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Noun.

00:23:36.960 --> 00:23:38.210 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: A noun, yeah.

00:23:38.210 --> 00:23:40.460 align:middle line:84%
"Treatment" and
"government" are both nouns.

00:23:40.460 --> 00:23:44.180 align:middle line:84%
And I'll put that
again over here.

00:23:44.180 --> 00:23:51.070 align:middle line:84%
Do-do-do, "-ment" attaches
to verbs and creates nouns.

00:23:51.070 --> 00:23:52.730 align:middle line:90%
OK, cool.

00:23:52.730 --> 00:23:59.750 align:middle line:84%
Now, how about the prefix "em-,"
as in "embody" and "empower."

00:23:59.750 --> 00:24:05.835 align:middle line:84%
What does "em-" attach to,
and what does it create?

00:24:05.835 --> 00:24:06.710 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:06.710 --> 00:24:08.127 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Attaches to nouns

00:24:08.127 --> 00:24:13.310 align:middle line:90%
and makes them into verbs, aha.

00:24:13.310 --> 00:24:16.270 align:middle line:84%
I'll just say that
again over here.

00:24:16.270 --> 00:24:19.510 align:middle line:84%
[LAUGHS] There's got to be
a better way to do this.

00:24:19.510 --> 00:24:24.450 align:middle line:84%
Attaches to nouns and
makes them into verbs.

00:24:24.450 --> 00:24:25.110 align:middle line:90%
OK, cool.

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:27.630 align:middle line:90%


00:24:27.630 --> 00:24:33.240 align:middle line:84%
All right, so we just said,
we know why you can't say

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:35.710 align:middle line:84%
"bodiment" or "powerment."
it's because "-ment"

00:24:35.710 --> 00:24:40.050 align:middle line:84%
attaches to verbs, and "body"
and "power" are not verbs.

00:24:40.050 --> 00:24:43.490 align:middle line:84%
How come you can say
"embodiment" and "empowerment"?

00:24:43.490 --> 00:24:45.575 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Because [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:45.575 --> 00:24:48.200 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: All of you are
saying the right thing, sort of

00:24:48.200 --> 00:24:48.950 align:middle line:90%
in chorus.

00:24:48.950 --> 00:24:50.510 align:middle line:84%
As the semester goes
along, eventually, you'll

00:24:50.510 --> 00:24:52.010 align:middle line:84%
start saying these
things in unison,

00:24:52.010 --> 00:24:53.840 align:middle line:90%
so it would be really cool.

00:24:53.840 --> 00:24:54.710 align:middle line:90%
Maybe with harmony.

00:24:54.710 --> 00:24:58.190 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, you can't
attach "-ment" to "body"

00:24:58.190 --> 00:25:00.050 align:middle line:90%
because "body" is a noun.

00:25:00.050 --> 00:25:03.270 align:middle line:84%
But you can attach
"-ment" to "embody."

00:25:03.270 --> 00:25:05.675 align:middle line:90%
So "embody" is a verb.

00:25:05.675 --> 00:25:07.160 align:middle line:90%
"Empower" is a verb.

00:25:07.160 --> 00:25:10.580 align:middle line:84%
"Em-" attaches to the noun
"body" or the noun "power"

00:25:10.580 --> 00:25:12.820 align:middle line:90%
and makes it into a verb.

00:25:12.820 --> 00:25:16.450 align:middle line:84%
And then, "-ment" attaches to
the resulting verb and makes it

00:25:16.450 --> 00:25:19.270 align:middle line:90%
into a noun again.

00:25:19.270 --> 00:25:21.140 align:middle line:84%
So it's useful to think
of these morphemes,

00:25:21.140 --> 00:25:23.200 align:middle line:84%
so words like "embodiment"
and "empowerment,"

00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:27.550 align:middle line:84%
not just as consisting of three
morphemes-- although they do,

00:25:27.550 --> 00:25:30.430 align:middle line:84%
a prefix, a stem,
and then a suffix--

00:25:30.430 --> 00:25:32.350 align:middle line:84%
but as consisting
in three morphemes

00:25:32.350 --> 00:25:35.770 align:middle line:84%
that are assembled in
a particular order.

00:25:35.770 --> 00:25:40.660 align:middle line:84%
You first attach the prefix,
and then you attach the suffix.

00:25:40.660 --> 00:25:43.040 align:middle line:84%
People see why I'm
saying it that way?

00:25:43.040 --> 00:25:45.020 align:middle line:90%
That make sense?

00:25:45.020 --> 00:25:52.050 align:middle line:84%
So here's a way of
representing that fact, which

00:25:52.050 --> 00:25:54.340 align:middle line:84%
is going to come in
handy as we go forward.

00:25:54.340 --> 00:25:57.480 align:middle line:84%
We're going to want to say,
yeah, you start with "power,"

00:25:57.480 --> 00:26:01.617 align:middle line:84%
and you glom "power"
together with this prefix.

00:26:01.617 --> 00:26:03.450 align:middle line:84%
I've given it the label
"aff," which is just

00:26:03.450 --> 00:26:05.580 align:middle line:90%
supposed to stand for "affix."

00:26:05.580 --> 00:26:09.180 align:middle line:84%
So the affix "em-,"
the prefix "em-",

00:26:09.180 --> 00:26:12.900 align:middle line:84%
attaches to the noun
"power" and creates a verb.

00:26:12.900 --> 00:26:17.310 align:middle line:84%
That's what the lower
left-hand side of that tree

00:26:17.310 --> 00:26:18.660 align:middle line:90%
is meant to show you.

00:26:18.660 --> 00:26:20.140 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:26:20.140 --> 00:26:24.090 align:middle line:84%
And then, that verb is combined
with this other affix, "-ment,"

00:26:24.090 --> 00:26:29.420 align:middle line:84%
and the result is a
noun, "empowerment."

00:26:29.420 --> 00:26:30.600 align:middle line:90%
That make sense?

00:26:30.600 --> 00:26:33.390 align:middle line:84%
So this tree is just a way of
representing what I just said,

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:35.390 align:middle line:84%
which is it's useful
to think of this

00:26:35.390 --> 00:26:39.320 align:middle line:84%
as not just a word consisting
of three morphemes,

00:26:39.320 --> 00:26:42.350 align:middle line:84%
but a word consisting
of three morphemes

00:26:42.350 --> 00:26:44.420 align:middle line:90%
which are attached in pairs.

00:26:44.420 --> 00:26:46.310 align:middle line:84%
That is, you attach
"em-" to "power,"

00:26:46.310 --> 00:26:48.650 align:middle line:84%
and then you attach
"-ment" to "empower."

00:26:48.650 --> 00:26:50.120 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:26:50.120 --> 00:26:52.610 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Are the placements
of the affixes on this tree

00:26:52.610 --> 00:26:54.980 align:middle line:84%
because of whether they're
prefixes or suffixes?

00:26:54.980 --> 00:26:55.970 align:middle line:90%
Yes, yeah.

00:26:55.970 --> 00:26:58.745 align:middle line:84%
So you're asking, why did
they put "em-" before "power,"

00:26:58.745 --> 00:27:00.620 align:middle line:84%
and why did they put
"-ment" after "empower"?

00:27:00.620 --> 00:27:03.260 align:middle line:84%
And yes, it's just whether
they're prefixes or suffixes.

00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:05.542 align:middle line:84%
That's all I was
trying to do, right?

00:27:05.542 --> 00:27:07.250 align:middle line:84%
So the tree is just
a way of representing

00:27:07.250 --> 00:27:11.500 align:middle line:84%
that kind of derivational
history of this word.

00:27:11.500 --> 00:27:13.830 align:middle line:84%
So what's the order
of operations that you

00:27:13.830 --> 00:27:15.120 align:middle line:90%
used to create this word?

00:27:15.120 --> 00:27:15.900 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:27:15.900 --> 00:27:18.442 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So they're ordered--
so it goes from the bottom up?

00:27:18.442 --> 00:27:20.940 align:middle line:84%
Yes, that's a way
to talk about it.

00:27:20.940 --> 00:27:23.680 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, OK.

00:27:23.680 --> 00:27:24.410 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:27:24.410 --> 00:27:27.280 align:middle line:90%
What [INAUDIBLE]?

00:27:27.280 --> 00:27:32.830 align:middle line:84%
Oh, well, infixes
would be where I would

00:27:32.830 --> 00:27:34.990 align:middle line:90%
fail at what I'm trying to do.

00:27:34.990 --> 00:27:36.850 align:middle line:84%
This was your point
from just now.

00:27:36.850 --> 00:27:40.060 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying to represent whether
things are prefixes or suffixes

00:27:40.060 --> 00:27:41.740 align:middle line:90%
by using the tree.

00:27:41.740 --> 00:27:44.470 align:middle line:84%
If we were doing
Tagalog, and I wanted

00:27:44.470 --> 00:27:48.670 align:middle line:84%
to do the past tense of the verb
"baba," which involves an infix

00:27:48.670 --> 00:27:54.550 align:middle line:84%
"um" I'd probably put
together, here's the affix,

00:27:54.550 --> 00:27:56.170 align:middle line:84%
and this was a
verb, and I'm going

00:27:56.170 --> 00:27:58.700 align:middle line:84%
to put these two things
together as a single thing.

00:27:58.700 --> 00:28:01.120 align:middle line:84%
And then, yes, somewhere
else, I would have to say, oh,

00:28:01.120 --> 00:28:02.980 align:middle line:90%
by the way, this is an infix.

00:28:02.980 --> 00:28:05.260 align:middle line:84%
So try not to take
too seriously which

00:28:05.260 --> 00:28:07.570 align:middle line:84%
things are to the left and
right of which other things.

00:28:07.570 --> 00:28:09.940 align:middle line:84%
What I'm really trying to
get across with these trees

00:28:09.940 --> 00:28:13.175 align:middle line:84%
is the order in which you
are putting things together.

00:28:13.175 --> 00:28:14.800 align:middle line:84%
The fact that some
things are prefixes,

00:28:14.800 --> 00:28:17.560 align:middle line:84%
and others are suffixes, and
others are infixes, or tones,

00:28:17.560 --> 00:28:21.550 align:middle line:84%
or whatever all else, that's
somebody else's job for today,

00:28:21.550 --> 00:28:24.000 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:26.880 align:middle line:90%
Right, OK.

00:28:26.880 --> 00:28:29.370 align:middle line:84%
There's a standard way of
talking about trees like this.

00:28:29.370 --> 00:28:31.235 align:middle line:84%
Trees like this
are very popular.

00:28:31.235 --> 00:28:32.610 align:middle line:84%
Try to get
comfortable with them.

00:28:32.610 --> 00:28:34.680 align:middle line:84%
We will spend lots of
time looking at trees

00:28:34.680 --> 00:28:36.665 align:middle line:90%
as the semester goes along.

00:28:36.665 --> 00:28:38.040 align:middle line:84%
There's a standard
way of talking

00:28:38.040 --> 00:28:41.550 align:middle line:84%
about them, which involves
feminine kinship terms.

00:28:41.550 --> 00:28:44.280 align:middle line:84%
So we'll say that
the sister of "em-"

00:28:44.280 --> 00:28:48.120 align:middle line:84%
is a noun because those two
things are next to each other

00:28:48.120 --> 00:28:48.810 align:middle line:90%
on the tree.

00:28:48.810 --> 00:28:53.477 align:middle line:84%
They are two daughters of the
same thing, that verb node

00:28:53.477 --> 00:28:54.435 align:middle line:90%
that's above them both.

00:28:54.435 --> 00:28:57.780 align:middle line:90%


00:28:57.780 --> 00:29:00.690 align:middle line:84%
Another way to say it is that
that verb is the mother of both

00:29:00.690 --> 00:29:03.330 align:middle line:90%
"em-" and "power."

00:29:03.330 --> 00:29:05.910 align:middle line:84%
I think that's as far as people
go with the feminine kinship

00:29:05.910 --> 00:29:06.410 align:middle line:90%
terms.

00:29:06.410 --> 00:29:08.940 align:middle line:84%
People don't talk about
"aunts," or whatever.

00:29:08.940 --> 00:29:12.528 align:middle line:84%
So it's "sisters," and
"daughters," and "mothers."

00:29:12.528 --> 00:29:14.070 align:middle line:84%
Nobody even talks
about grandmothers.

00:29:14.070 --> 00:29:16.170 align:middle line:90%
It's nuclear families.

00:29:16.170 --> 00:29:18.342 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so there's
just a terminology

00:29:18.342 --> 00:29:19.300 align:middle line:90%
for talking about this.

00:29:19.300 --> 00:29:24.360 align:middle line:84%
So "-ment" has a verb as its
sister, and its mother is in N.

00:29:24.360 --> 00:29:29.820 align:middle line:84%
When we say "-ment" has a verb
as its sister and a noun as its

00:29:29.820 --> 00:29:32.280 align:middle line:84%
mother, that's a way of
saying things like what I have

00:29:32.280 --> 00:29:34.592 align:middle line:90%
on the board here twice--

00:29:34.592 --> 00:29:37.050 align:middle line:84%
that "-ment" is something that
takes a verb and converts it

00:29:37.050 --> 00:29:38.100 align:middle line:90%
into a noun.

00:29:38.100 --> 00:29:40.920 align:middle line:84%
That's how that kind of fact
is represented in these trees.

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:43.570 align:middle line:90%


00:29:43.570 --> 00:29:50.090 align:middle line:90%
OK, now there will be times--

00:29:50.090 --> 00:29:53.630 align:middle line:84%
big surprise, this
is like day three.

00:29:53.630 --> 00:29:56.060 align:middle line:84%
I've been looking at
comparatively simple cases.

00:29:56.060 --> 00:29:57.560 align:middle line:84%
There are going to
be examples where

00:29:57.560 --> 00:30:00.320 align:middle line:84%
we'll want to distinguish
multiple affixes that are maybe

00:30:00.320 --> 00:30:01.410 align:middle line:90%
similar to each other.

00:30:01.410 --> 00:30:05.090 align:middle line:90%
So think about the prefix "un-".

00:30:05.090 --> 00:30:07.610 align:middle line:84%
The prefix "un-" shows
up in words like "unwrap"

00:30:07.610 --> 00:30:12.680 align:middle line:84%
and "untie," and also in words
like "unlikely" and "unhappy."

00:30:12.680 --> 00:30:19.140 align:middle line:84%
yeah these two "un-"s arguably
mean different things,

00:30:19.140 --> 00:30:22.030 align:middle line:84%
and they're attaching
to different things.

00:30:22.030 --> 00:30:23.850 align:middle line:84%
So somebody help
me be less vague.

00:30:23.850 --> 00:30:25.170 align:middle line:90%
What's the first "un-" doing?

00:30:25.170 --> 00:30:26.490 align:middle line:90%
What does it attach to?

00:30:26.490 --> 00:30:29.193 align:middle line:90%


00:30:29.193 --> 00:30:29.860 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Verbs.

00:30:29.860 --> 00:30:30.430 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Verbs.

00:30:30.430 --> 00:30:32.560 align:middle line:84%
OK, so there's an "un-"
that attaches to verbs.

00:30:32.560 --> 00:30:34.720 align:middle line:84%
What does the "un-" that
attaches to verbs mean?

00:30:34.720 --> 00:30:38.360 align:middle line:90%


00:30:38.360 --> 00:30:40.410 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: An undoing
of that verb.

00:30:40.410 --> 00:30:43.020 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, you
undo-- wait, I just used--

00:30:43.020 --> 00:30:44.630 align:middle line:90%
yeah, you reverse.

00:30:44.630 --> 00:30:45.130 align:middle line:90%
There we go.

00:30:45.130 --> 00:30:48.022 align:middle line:84%
You take the result
state of the verb,

00:30:48.022 --> 00:30:49.730 align:middle line:84%
and you cause it to
go back to the way it

00:30:49.730 --> 00:30:51.938 align:middle line:84%
was before the verb was
done, or something like that.

00:30:51.938 --> 00:30:53.810 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so if you
unwrap something,

00:30:53.810 --> 00:30:55.490 align:middle line:84%
you take something
which is wrapped,

00:30:55.490 --> 00:30:58.070 align:middle line:84%
and you cause it to
not be wrapped anymore,

00:30:58.070 --> 00:30:59.220 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:00.380 align:middle line:90%
Same with "untie."

00:31:00.380 --> 00:31:06.950 align:middle line:84%
So there's an "un-" that
attaches to verbs and means

00:31:06.950 --> 00:31:09.620 align:middle line:90%
something like "reverse."

00:31:09.620 --> 00:31:15.680 align:middle line:84%
Or, to put it another
way, there's an "un-"

00:31:15.680 --> 00:31:20.373 align:middle line:84%
that attaches to verbs and
means something like "reverse."

00:31:20.373 --> 00:31:22.040 align:middle line:84%
That's actually putting
it the same way,

00:31:22.040 --> 00:31:23.990 align:middle line:90%
but in a different place, yeah?

00:31:23.990 --> 00:31:26.360 align:middle line:84%
And then, there's another
"un-" that attaches to what?

00:31:26.360 --> 00:31:29.530 align:middle line:90%
What's the other "un-"?

00:31:29.530 --> 00:31:31.604 align:middle line:90%
What does it attach to?

00:31:31.604 --> 00:31:32.479 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Adjectives.

00:31:32.479 --> 00:31:34.604 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Adjectives,
and what does it mean?

00:31:34.604 --> 00:31:35.310 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Not."

00:31:35.310 --> 00:31:38.400 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, like
"not," or something like that.

00:31:38.400 --> 00:31:44.400 align:middle line:90%


00:31:44.400 --> 00:31:48.330 align:middle line:84%
I guess we get into debates
about whether "unhappy"

00:31:48.330 --> 00:31:55.500 align:middle line:84%
and "sad" mean the same
thing, or "unlikely"

00:31:55.500 --> 00:31:58.172 align:middle line:84%
and whatever the
opposite of "likely" is.

00:31:58.172 --> 00:31:59.130 align:middle line:90%
What am I putting here?

00:31:59.130 --> 00:32:05.340 align:middle line:90%
Adjectives, "not."

00:32:05.340 --> 00:32:06.320 align:middle line:90%
So two "un-"s.

00:32:06.320 --> 00:32:07.680 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:32:07.680 --> 00:32:11.250 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If you a word like
"unwrapping," is the "wrapping"

00:32:11.250 --> 00:32:14.340 align:middle line:84%
a participle and "un-" a third
"un-," or is it "unwrap,"

00:32:14.340 --> 00:32:16.440 align:middle line:84%
and it's "-ing"
modifying the "unwrap"?

00:32:16.440 --> 00:32:17.320 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Really good question.

00:32:17.320 --> 00:32:18.420 align:middle line:84%
What kind of tree
should we draw?

00:32:18.420 --> 00:32:19.410 align:middle line:90%
Should we draw a tree?

00:32:19.410 --> 00:32:21.720 align:middle line:84%
So the question was
for "unwrapping,"

00:32:21.720 --> 00:32:24.960 align:middle line:84%
are we going to attach "un-" to
"wrap," giving a verb "unwrap,"

00:32:24.960 --> 00:32:26.400 align:middle line:90%
and add "-ing" to that?

00:32:26.400 --> 00:32:29.820 align:middle line:84%
Or are we going to add "-ing" to
"wrap," giving you "wrapping,"

00:32:29.820 --> 00:32:32.815 align:middle line:84%
and then add "un-" to
that, a third "un-?

00:32:32.815 --> 00:32:33.690 align:middle line:90%
What do people think?

00:32:33.690 --> 00:32:34.440 align:middle line:90%
What should we do?

00:32:34.440 --> 00:32:37.120 align:middle line:90%


00:32:37.120 --> 00:32:39.620 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It depends on what
you're using the word as, yeah.

00:32:39.620 --> 00:32:40.698 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah?

00:32:40.698 --> 00:32:41.240 align:middle line:90%
Do you have--

00:32:41.240 --> 00:32:43.430 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If you're using
the word as the verb,

00:32:43.430 --> 00:32:47.720 align:middle line:84%
"I am unwrapping this thing,"
then you would do the first,

00:32:47.720 --> 00:32:50.480 align:middle line:84%
where you attach
the "-ing" to "wrap"

00:32:50.480 --> 00:32:53.240 align:middle line:90%
and you undo the wrapping.

00:32:53.240 --> 00:32:56.510 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, so
well, we have an "un-"--

00:32:56.510 --> 00:32:58.190 align:middle line:84%
maybe this is a way
to think about it.

00:32:58.190 --> 00:33:01.460 align:middle line:84%
We have an "un-" that
we can attach to verbs.

00:33:01.460 --> 00:33:03.020 align:middle line:84%
And so in the case
of "unwrapping,"

00:33:03.020 --> 00:33:06.330 align:middle line:84%
I guess we could hope that
we can survive with just that

00:33:06.330 --> 00:33:09.110 align:middle line:84%
"un-" until something forces
us to do something else.

00:33:09.110 --> 00:33:10.310 align:middle line:90%
But you have an idea, too.

00:33:10.310 --> 00:33:14.093 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
verb, like "unwrapping"?

00:33:14.093 --> 00:33:15.010 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:33:15.010 --> 00:33:16.640 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Then, then "unwrap"
[INAUDIBLE] the "ing" part

00:33:16.640 --> 00:33:17.432 align:middle line:90%
of it, [INAUDIBLE]?

00:33:17.432 --> 00:33:18.348 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:33:18.348 --> 00:33:18.848 align:middle line:90%


00:33:18.848 --> 00:33:21.778 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] something
[INAUDIBLE] of the work?

00:33:21.778 --> 00:33:22.653 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

00:33:22.653 --> 00:33:28.460 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
to now, and [INAUDIBLE]??

00:33:28.460 --> 00:33:30.460 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So I
guess there are two words.

00:33:30.460 --> 00:33:31.570 align:middle line:84%
Unwrapping, this
was your point, too.

00:33:31.570 --> 00:33:33.160 align:middle line:84%
There are two
words, "unwrapping,"

00:33:33.160 --> 00:33:34.285 align:middle line:90%
that we should think about.

00:33:34.285 --> 00:33:36.580 align:middle line:84%
One is, "He is
unwrapping the presents,"

00:33:36.580 --> 00:33:39.973 align:middle line:84%
and the other is,
"Unwrapping presents is fun,"

00:33:39.973 --> 00:33:41.890 align:middle line:84%
where "Unwrapping presents
is fun," hopefully,

00:33:41.890 --> 00:33:43.307 align:middle line:84%
"unwrapping" is
some kind of noun.

00:33:43.307 --> 00:33:47.600 align:middle line:84%
It's what people sometimes call
a gerund, where you convert

00:33:47.600 --> 00:33:50.830 align:middle line:84%
a verb into a noun that means
the process of doing the verb,

00:33:50.830 --> 00:33:52.420 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:33:52.420 --> 00:33:54.850 align:middle line:84%
Maybe in both cases,
we would hope, yeah,

00:33:54.850 --> 00:33:58.390 align:middle line:84%
that you would first
create the "un-" verb,

00:33:58.390 --> 00:34:01.150 align:middle line:84%
and then you would add
"-ing" to it, because "-ing"

00:34:01.150 --> 00:34:03.977 align:middle line:84%
attaches to verbs and does other
things with them sometimes.

00:34:03.977 --> 00:34:04.810 align:middle line:90%
They're still verbs.

00:34:04.810 --> 00:34:07.052 align:middle line:90%
Sometimes they're nouns now.

00:34:07.052 --> 00:34:09.010 align:middle line:84%
That would be my first
hope until somebody made

00:34:09.010 --> 00:34:10.659 align:middle line:90%
me hope for something else.

00:34:10.659 --> 00:34:16.810 align:middle line:84%
Maybe one way to say this is,
these are cases where we know

00:34:16.810 --> 00:34:19.659 align:middle line:84%
that there have
to be two "un-"s,

00:34:19.659 --> 00:34:22.449 align:middle line:84%
both because these two "un-"s
seem to mean slightly different

00:34:22.449 --> 00:34:24.157 align:middle line:84%
things, and because,
well, they're just--

00:34:24.157 --> 00:34:26.770 align:middle line:84%
they are attaching to
things of different kinds,

00:34:26.770 --> 00:34:27.880 align:middle line:90%
verbs and adjectives.

00:34:27.880 --> 00:34:30.400 align:middle line:90%


00:34:30.400 --> 00:34:32.620 align:middle line:84%
Your example, where
we're adding "un-"

00:34:32.620 --> 00:34:36.760 align:middle line:84%
to-- so suppose we were thinking
about the "unwrapping is fun"

00:34:36.760 --> 00:34:38.810 align:middle line:90%
case, the gerund case.

00:34:38.810 --> 00:34:41.440 align:middle line:84%
If you wanted to add "un-"
after you added "ing,"

00:34:41.440 --> 00:34:44.315 align:middle line:84%
after you made it into a noun,
you'd be invoking an "un-"

00:34:44.315 --> 00:34:46.510 align:middle line:90%
that attaches to nouns.

00:34:46.510 --> 00:34:49.600 align:middle line:84%
And then the question, the next
question people would ask you,

00:34:49.600 --> 00:34:52.330 align:middle line:84%
would be, OK, so can you show
me an example of "un" attaching

00:34:52.330 --> 00:34:55.960 align:middle line:84%
to a noun where the noun isn't
created from a verb with "ing,"

00:34:55.960 --> 00:34:57.067 align:middle line:90%
just a plain noun?

00:34:57.067 --> 00:34:58.900 align:middle line:84%
That would be the
strongest kind of evidence

00:34:58.900 --> 00:35:00.400 align:middle line:90%
that we needed a third "un".

00:35:00.400 --> 00:35:03.300 align:middle line:90%
Does that make any sense?

00:35:03.300 --> 00:35:04.620 align:middle line:90%
Good question.

00:35:04.620 --> 00:35:06.910 align:middle line:90%
Other questions?

00:35:06.910 --> 00:35:12.338 align:middle line:90%
OK, all right, so we need--

00:35:12.338 --> 00:35:14.380 align:middle line:84%
what does it [INAUDIBLE]
to, what does it create?

00:35:14.380 --> 00:35:16.705 align:middle line:90%
OK, cool, so we need two "un-"s.

00:35:16.705 --> 00:35:18.580 align:middle line:84%
Here's another affix to
think about: "-able."

00:35:18.580 --> 00:35:20.950 align:middle line:84%
"Drinkable," "breakable,"
"watchable," what does "-able"

00:35:20.950 --> 00:35:22.683 align:middle line:90%
attach to?

00:35:22.683 --> 00:35:23.350 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Verbs.

00:35:23.350 --> 00:35:25.550 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Verbs,
and what do you get?

00:35:25.550 --> 00:35:26.530 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Adjectives.

00:35:26.530 --> 00:35:27.947 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Adjectives, yeah.

00:35:27.947 --> 00:35:43.310 align:middle line:84%
So "-able," "-able" converts
verbs into adjectives,

00:35:43.310 --> 00:35:52.460 align:middle line:84%
and it also converts
verbs into adjectives.

00:35:52.460 --> 00:35:55.510 align:middle line:84%
"Un-" doesn't convert the things
that attaches to at all so far,

00:35:55.510 --> 00:35:56.010 align:middle line:90%
yeah?

00:35:56.010 --> 00:35:58.220 align:middle line:84%
So "un-"-- we'll
go back to "un-."

00:35:58.220 --> 00:35:59.870 align:middle line:90%
Sure, we will-- there we go.

00:35:59.870 --> 00:36:02.090 align:middle line:84%
"Un-" attaches to verbs,
and what you get is a verb.

00:36:02.090 --> 00:36:03.740 align:middle line:84%
"Un-" attaches to adjectives,
and what you get is

00:36:03.740 --> 00:36:04.350 align:middle line:90%
an adjective.

00:36:04.350 --> 00:36:05.930 align:middle line:84%
So it changes the meaning,
but it doesn't change

00:36:05.930 --> 00:36:07.013 align:middle line:90%
the category of the word.

00:36:07.013 --> 00:36:08.180 align:middle line:90%
The thing is still the same.

00:36:08.180 --> 00:36:10.030 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, OK.

00:36:10.030 --> 00:36:13.820 align:middle line:90%


00:36:13.820 --> 00:36:18.260 align:middle line:84%
OK so we have "un-"s, one that
attaches to verbs and makes new

00:36:18.260 --> 00:36:21.530 align:middle line:84%
verbs-- yes, thank
you, I just said that--

00:36:21.530 --> 00:36:24.260 align:middle line:84%
and then another one that
attaches to adjectives

00:36:24.260 --> 00:36:26.680 align:middle line:90%
and makes new adjectives.

00:36:26.680 --> 00:36:29.180 align:middle line:84%
And then we have an "-able"
that attaches to verbs and makes

00:36:29.180 --> 00:36:31.720 align:middle line:90%
adjectives, yeah?

00:36:31.720 --> 00:36:33.050 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:36:33.050 --> 00:36:38.210 align:middle line:84%
So now let's think about a
case where we're adding "un-"

00:36:38.210 --> 00:36:42.710 align:middle line:84%
and also "-able," a
word like "unlockable."

00:36:42.710 --> 00:36:45.470 align:middle line:84%
What should that
be able to mean?

00:36:45.470 --> 00:36:46.820 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:36:46.820 --> 00:36:49.350 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yes, it's the answer.

00:36:49.350 --> 00:36:53.770 align:middle line:84%
So if we're going to
start with "lock"--

00:36:53.770 --> 00:36:55.580 align:middle line:90%
let me find an eraser.

00:36:55.580 --> 00:36:59.760 align:middle line:90%


00:36:59.760 --> 00:37:03.036 align:middle line:84%
So we'll do this
over here first.

00:37:03.036 --> 00:37:07.470 align:middle line:84%
If we're going to start
with "lock," what shall

00:37:07.470 --> 00:37:09.855 align:middle line:90%
we add first?

00:37:09.855 --> 00:37:11.160 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Un-."

00:37:11.160 --> 00:37:14.010 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "Un-," let's
say, OK, so "lock" is a verb.

00:37:14.010 --> 00:37:15.150 align:middle line:90%
We'll add "un-."

00:37:15.150 --> 00:37:17.468 align:middle line:90%
We'll get-- so here's an affix.

00:37:17.468 --> 00:37:20.010 align:middle line:84%
Put these two things together,
what will the result of adding

00:37:20.010 --> 00:37:21.512 align:middle line:90%
"un-" to "lock" be?

00:37:21.512 --> 00:37:22.220 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: A verb.

00:37:22.220 --> 00:37:25.470 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Another verb, yeah.

00:37:25.470 --> 00:37:29.400 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll take "-able,"
which we know attaches to verbs

00:37:29.400 --> 00:37:32.950 align:middle line:90%
and creates adjectives.

00:37:32.950 --> 00:37:36.310 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, so we'll take that affix.

00:37:36.310 --> 00:37:37.480 align:middle line:90%
We'll attach another verb.

00:37:37.480 --> 00:37:43.370 align:middle line:84%
And what we'll get
will be an adjective.

00:37:43.370 --> 00:37:46.100 align:middle line:84%
So we'll have an adjective which
we created by first putting

00:37:46.100 --> 00:37:50.550 align:middle line:84%
"un-" on "lock," and then
putting "-able" on "unlock."

00:37:50.550 --> 00:37:54.690 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to draw the
other option over here.

00:37:54.690 --> 00:37:57.920 align:middle line:90%
And where am I going to put it?

00:37:57.920 --> 00:37:59.870 align:middle line:90%
Here.

00:37:59.870 --> 00:38:03.880 align:middle line:84%
So over there, we attached
"un-" first, so this time,

00:38:03.880 --> 00:38:06.160 align:middle line:84%
we're going to take
the verb "lock",

00:38:06.160 --> 00:38:07.780 align:middle line:84%
and we're going to
add "-able" to it.

00:38:07.780 --> 00:38:11.360 align:middle line:90%


00:38:11.360 --> 00:38:14.810 align:middle line:84%
What's the result going to be
when we add the affix "-able"

00:38:14.810 --> 00:38:17.400 align:middle line:90%
to the verb "lock"?

00:38:17.400 --> 00:38:19.950 align:middle line:90%
An adjective.

00:38:19.950 --> 00:38:24.357 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to add the
affix "un-" to that adjective,

00:38:24.357 --> 00:38:26.190 align:middle line:84%
and the result of that
will be an adjective.

00:38:26.190 --> 00:38:30.250 align:middle line:84%
Both of these will be
pronounced "unlockable."

00:38:30.250 --> 00:38:32.650 align:middle line:90%
But what will they mean?

00:38:32.650 --> 00:38:35.844 align:middle line:90%
What will that one mean?

00:38:35.844 --> 00:38:37.558 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Not able to be locked.

00:38:37.558 --> 00:38:39.100 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: It
cannot be locked.

00:38:39.100 --> 00:38:41.440 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, so this is a broken lock.

00:38:41.440 --> 00:38:44.070 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, it is unlockable.

00:38:44.070 --> 00:38:47.730 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, right, because when
you add "-able" to "lock,"

00:38:47.730 --> 00:38:50.880 align:middle line:90%
you get an adjective--

00:38:50.880 --> 00:38:52.523 align:middle line:90%
possible to lock this thing.

00:38:52.523 --> 00:38:53.940 align:middle line:84%
And then, when you
add "un" to it,

00:38:53.940 --> 00:38:57.050 align:middle line:84%
you say not possible
to lock this thing.

00:38:57.050 --> 00:38:59.170 align:middle line:84%
How about that one, that
"unlockable" over there,

00:38:59.170 --> 00:39:01.207 align:middle line:84%
the one where we first
added "un-" to "lock"

00:39:01.207 --> 00:39:02.290 align:middle line:90%
and then we added "-able?"

00:39:02.290 --> 00:39:03.873 align:middle line:90%
What does this one mean?

00:39:03.873 --> 00:39:05.040 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Can be unlocked."

00:39:05.040 --> 00:39:07.300 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
"Can be unlocked."

00:39:07.300 --> 00:39:11.730 align:middle line:84%
This is not a broken lock, a
lock that's working properly.

00:39:11.730 --> 00:39:14.820 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, and that's our intuition
about the word "unlockable."

00:39:14.820 --> 00:39:17.510 align:middle line:90%
It is ambiguous.

00:39:17.510 --> 00:39:20.100 align:middle line:84%
If you say, this
door is unlockable,

00:39:20.100 --> 00:39:26.600 align:middle line:84%
because I have the key, or
this door is unlockable,

00:39:26.600 --> 00:39:29.130 align:middle line:90%
somebody calls a locksmith.

00:39:29.130 --> 00:39:30.060 align:middle line:90%
Does that sound right?

00:39:30.060 --> 00:39:32.410 align:middle line:84%
It can be either
of those things.

00:39:32.410 --> 00:39:32.910 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:39:32.910 --> 00:39:35.215 align:middle line:90%


00:39:35.215 --> 00:39:38.980 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I [INAUDIBLE],,
I don't think

00:39:38.980 --> 00:39:44.420 align:middle line:84%
there is a particular
[INAUDIBLE] on [INAUDIBLE]..

00:39:44.420 --> 00:39:46.510 align:middle line:84%
If we looked at
"unsinkable," for instance--

00:39:46.510 --> 00:39:47.427 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah?

00:39:47.427 --> 00:39:50.330 align:middle line:90%


00:39:50.330 --> 00:39:55.730 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I don't tend to hear
"unsinkable" as meaning "able

00:39:55.730 --> 00:39:56.910 align:middle line:90%
to be unsunk."

00:39:56.910 --> 00:39:59.740 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Right, do you think

00:39:59.740 --> 00:40:03.678 align:middle line:84%
do you think there
is a verb "unsink"?

00:40:03.678 --> 00:40:04.890 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I don't know.

00:40:04.890 --> 00:40:06.640 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah,
so actually, you're

00:40:06.640 --> 00:40:07.485 align:middle line:90%
raising-- oh, yes.

00:40:07.485 --> 00:40:09.652 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Does that have
to do with the transitivity

00:40:09.652 --> 00:40:11.110 align:middle line:90%
of the verb?

00:40:11.110 --> 00:40:13.820 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Well,
you can sink a ship,

00:40:13.820 --> 00:40:15.770 align:middle line:90%
so the verb's transitive.

00:40:15.770 --> 00:40:20.430 align:middle line:84%
And the ship can also sink,
so it can be intransitive.

00:40:20.430 --> 00:40:22.920 align:middle line:84%
You can lock a door,
and a door can lock.

00:40:22.920 --> 00:40:24.420 align:middle line:84%
So we have a bunch
of verbs that can

00:40:24.420 --> 00:40:26.920 align:middle line:84%
go back and forth between being
transitive and intransitive.

00:40:26.920 --> 00:40:28.960 align:middle line:84%
And we'll talk more
about transitivity later.

00:40:28.960 --> 00:40:30.660 align:middle line:84%
But for people who are
wondering what the heck we're

00:40:30.660 --> 00:40:32.790 align:middle line:84%
talking about, verbs are
said to be transitive

00:40:32.790 --> 00:40:34.630 align:middle line:84%
if they have both a
subject and an object.

00:40:34.630 --> 00:40:36.660 align:middle line:84%
So if you sink a ship,
"I sank the ship,"

00:40:36.660 --> 00:40:38.640 align:middle line:90%
then the verb is transitive.

00:40:38.640 --> 00:40:41.260 align:middle line:84%
If you say, "the ship sank,"
then there's only a subject.

00:40:41.260 --> 00:40:42.010 align:middle line:90%
There's no object.

00:40:42.010 --> 00:40:43.290 align:middle line:90%
So the verb is intransitive.

00:40:43.290 --> 00:40:45.780 align:middle line:84%
English has a lot of
verbs that can be either.

00:40:45.780 --> 00:40:48.390 align:middle line:84%
And a lot of the groups that
are up here can be either.

00:40:48.390 --> 00:40:51.330 align:middle line:84%
Your question raises a really
good point, though, which is,

00:40:51.330 --> 00:40:53.820 align:middle line:84%
I just casually said, you
can add "un-" to verbs,

00:40:53.820 --> 00:40:56.280 align:middle line:84%
and it means undo the
effect of the verb--

00:40:56.280 --> 00:40:59.850 align:middle line:84%
yeah, look, "unwrap,"
"untie," yeah.

00:40:59.850 --> 00:41:03.270 align:middle line:90%
But you can't unsink a ship.

00:41:03.270 --> 00:41:07.980 align:middle line:84%
Even If you go down with
divers, and find the ship,

00:41:07.980 --> 00:41:10.140 align:middle line:84%
and bring it back
to the surface,

00:41:10.140 --> 00:41:13.890 align:middle line:84%
you're still not unsinking
the ship, I don't think.

00:41:13.890 --> 00:41:17.010 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't matter how
good you are at this.

00:41:17.010 --> 00:41:20.070 align:middle line:84%
So we have to say some other
things about that "un-."

00:41:20.070 --> 00:41:21.870 align:middle line:90%
You're raising a good point.

00:41:21.870 --> 00:41:25.470 align:middle line:84%
There are some verbs that
cannot be reversed, and "sink"

00:41:25.470 --> 00:41:26.760 align:middle line:90%
maybe is one of them.

00:41:26.760 --> 00:41:29.580 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE], Joseph, yeah?

00:41:29.580 --> 00:41:32.250 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: This might just
be me, but I feel like

00:41:32.250 --> 00:41:36.360 align:middle line:84%
because the word is ambiguous,
at least in the context of that

00:41:36.360 --> 00:41:41.100 align:middle line:84%
word, I feel like in my mind,
my brain has decided that

00:41:41.100 --> 00:41:46.130 align:middle line:84%
"unlockable" means something
that generally this one,

00:41:46.130 --> 00:41:48.072 align:middle line:90%
like it cannot [INAUDIBLE].

00:41:48.072 --> 00:41:49.768 align:middle line:90%


00:41:49.768 --> 00:41:51.060 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: The other one?

00:41:51.060 --> 00:41:53.100 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah.

00:41:53.100 --> 00:41:55.050 align:middle line:84%
If your brain is like
my brain, then it really

00:41:55.050 --> 00:41:56.460 align:middle line:84%
likes that one, the
one you can't see.

00:41:56.460 --> 00:41:58.210 align:middle line:84%
It's not your fault.
Yeah, it's over there

00:41:58.210 --> 00:42:00.240 align:middle line:90%
under the slide, yep.

00:42:00.240 --> 00:42:02.410 align:middle line:90%
Yep, yep.

00:42:02.410 --> 00:42:05.145 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's like something
that can be unlocked, yeah.

00:42:05.145 --> 00:42:07.020 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah,
yeah, it's unlockable.

00:42:07.020 --> 00:42:08.010 align:middle line:90%
The door is unlockable.

00:42:08.010 --> 00:42:09.420 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:42:09.420 --> 00:42:10.860 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It's unlockable?

00:42:10.860 --> 00:42:11.770 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yep.

00:42:11.770 --> 00:42:13.500 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So you can
say the lock is broken.

00:42:13.500 --> 00:42:15.000 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, yeah, I might

00:42:15.000 --> 00:42:16.255 align:middle line:90%
be more likely to say that.

00:42:16.255 --> 00:42:17.130 align:middle line:90%
I think you're right.

00:42:17.130 --> 00:42:18.300 align:middle line:90%
I have that feeling too.

00:42:18.300 --> 00:42:21.665 align:middle line:84%
And actually, I cheated a second
ago when I said "unlockable."

00:42:21.665 --> 00:42:22.290 align:middle line:90%
It's ambiguous.

00:42:22.290 --> 00:42:23.560 align:middle line:90%
You can say either one.

00:42:23.560 --> 00:42:28.290 align:middle line:84%
But for me, at least, if
I want to mean that one--

00:42:28.290 --> 00:42:32.560 align:middle line:84%
the one that means it's
broken, you can't lock it--

00:42:32.560 --> 00:42:35.550 align:middle line:84%
I kind have to say
it's "un-lockable,"

00:42:35.550 --> 00:42:38.160 align:middle line:84%
like as opposed to this
one, which you say,

00:42:38.160 --> 00:42:39.870 align:middle line:90%
it's "unlockable."

00:42:39.870 --> 00:42:42.690 align:middle line:84%
I kind have to put another
stress on "un" or something

00:42:42.690 --> 00:42:43.350 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:42:43.350 --> 00:42:44.125 align:middle line:90%
That's my feeling.

00:42:44.125 --> 00:42:45.750 align:middle line:84%
But maybe some of
you are looking at me

00:42:45.750 --> 00:42:48.600 align:middle line:84%
as though I've grown two
heads, which is a kind of look

00:42:48.600 --> 00:42:51.300 align:middle line:84%
that I get a lot just kind of
walking around so, you know,

00:42:51.300 --> 00:42:52.290 align:middle line:90%
I'm used to it.

00:42:52.290 --> 00:42:53.790 align:middle line:84%
Does anybody else
have that feeling?

00:42:53.790 --> 00:42:55.350 align:middle line:84%
I don't think I
pronounce these two

00:42:55.350 --> 00:42:59.560 align:middle line:84%
verbs the same way, these two
adjectives in the same way.

00:42:59.560 --> 00:43:00.420 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:43:00.420 --> 00:43:04.800 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think
[INAUDIBLE] put a stress

00:43:04.800 --> 00:43:06.360 align:middle line:90%
in the word [INAUDIBLE].

00:43:06.360 --> 00:43:07.170 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:43:07.170 --> 00:43:10.285 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE] not [INAUDIBLE]

00:43:10.285 --> 00:43:11.160 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yep.

00:43:11.160 --> 00:43:12.072 align:middle line:90%


00:43:12.072 --> 00:43:13.900 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:43:13.900 --> 00:43:17.470 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes,
now this is 24.900.

00:43:17.470 --> 00:43:19.480 align:middle line:84%
We will surely not get
to the bottom of this.

00:43:19.480 --> 00:43:22.210 align:middle line:84%
But this is a really
interesting sort of question.

00:43:22.210 --> 00:43:24.010 align:middle line:84%
And maybe one of the
things we're learning

00:43:24.010 --> 00:43:26.260 align:middle line:84%
is that if we want to have
a full understanding of how

00:43:26.260 --> 00:43:28.330 align:middle line:84%
stress works, we need
to be willing to think

00:43:28.330 --> 00:43:29.870 align:middle line:90%
about these structures.

00:43:29.870 --> 00:43:32.620 align:middle line:84%
These structures are apparently
informing our intuitions

00:43:32.620 --> 00:43:34.592 align:middle line:84%
about where stress goes
and how it's treated,

00:43:34.592 --> 00:43:35.800 align:middle line:90%
which is kind of interesting.

00:43:35.800 --> 00:43:38.050 align:middle line:84%
You had a hand up a while
ago, and I never got to you.

00:43:38.050 --> 00:43:39.520 align:middle line:90%
I'm sorry.

00:43:39.520 --> 00:43:42.040 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: In the sentence,
you can't "unsink a ship,"

00:43:42.040 --> 00:43:42.410 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah?

00:43:42.410 --> 00:43:43.930 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Sink
does sound correct.

00:43:43.930 --> 00:43:44.290 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh.

00:43:44.290 --> 00:43:45.850 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: That's the previous--

00:43:45.850 --> 00:43:49.070 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: You
can't unsink a ship?

00:43:49.070 --> 00:43:49.765 align:middle line:90%
Oh, boy.

00:43:49.765 --> 00:43:52.720 align:middle line:90%


00:43:52.720 --> 00:43:55.030 align:middle line:84%
In general, you
can't unsink a ship,

00:43:55.030 --> 00:43:58.870 align:middle line:84%
but I think this ship
might be unsinkable.

00:43:58.870 --> 00:44:00.160 align:middle line:90%
Maybe, I don't know.

00:44:00.160 --> 00:44:03.250 align:middle line:90%


00:44:03.250 --> 00:44:06.650 align:middle line:90%
You can't you unburn a letter.

00:44:06.650 --> 00:44:09.450 align:middle line:90%
You can't unbreak a teapot.

00:44:09.450 --> 00:44:09.950 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:44:09.950 --> 00:44:15.612 align:middle line:84%
[LAUGHS] So maybe I was
too fast to say there are

00:44:15.612 --> 00:44:16.820 align:middle line:90%
verbs that can't be reversed.

00:44:16.820 --> 00:44:18.980 align:middle line:84%
And to the extent that
you can reverse them,

00:44:18.980 --> 00:44:22.230 align:middle line:84%
I think you then get
both of these adjectives.

00:44:22.230 --> 00:44:22.730 align:middle line:90%
So--

00:44:22.730 --> 00:44:24.313 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
certain affects.

00:44:24.313 --> 00:44:26.090 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:44:26.090 --> 00:44:27.470 align:middle line:90%
Whoo, these are deep waters.

00:44:27.470 --> 00:44:29.360 align:middle line:84%
Fortunately for me, other
people are raising their hands.

00:44:29.360 --> 00:44:30.380 align:middle line:84%
Let me see what
they have to say.

00:44:30.380 --> 00:44:31.040 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:44:31.040 --> 00:44:33.747 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think
it's like you're

00:44:33.747 --> 00:44:35.330 align:middle line:84%
pointing out in the
sentence, the fact

00:44:35.330 --> 00:44:36.940 align:middle line:90%
that it's not the same.

00:44:36.940 --> 00:44:37.550 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Oh, that's true.

00:44:37.550 --> 00:44:39.425 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You're pointing
out in the sentence

00:44:39.425 --> 00:44:41.400 align:middle line:84%
like, almost like that
sounds ridiculous.

00:44:41.400 --> 00:44:42.600 align:middle line:90%
You can't unsink a ship.

00:44:42.600 --> 00:44:43.250 align:middle line:90%
That's not going to work.

00:44:43.250 --> 00:44:44.667 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:44:44.667 --> 00:44:46.730 align:middle line:90%


00:44:46.730 --> 00:44:49.130 align:middle line:84%
"We have this crazy
scientist at MIT.

00:44:49.130 --> 00:44:52.070 align:middle line:84%
He's doing this weird
diving research."

00:44:52.070 --> 00:44:54.560 align:middle line:84%
He's spending all his time
trying to unsink ships.

00:44:54.560 --> 00:44:56.208 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:44:56.208 --> 00:44:56.750 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:44:56.750 --> 00:44:58.867 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it's just a matter
of the number of times

00:44:58.867 --> 00:45:00.200 align:middle line:90%
you say it, so that eventually--

00:45:00.200 --> 00:45:00.770 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:00.770 --> 00:45:04.460 align:middle line:84%
It's just unlocking a door,
this is the kind of thing that

00:45:04.460 --> 00:45:07.190 align:middle line:84%
happens all the time,
whereas unwrapping a present,

00:45:07.190 --> 00:45:10.020 align:middle line:84%
whereas unsinking a ship,
people generally don't.

00:45:10.020 --> 00:45:11.690 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, maybe that's
all that's happening.

00:45:11.690 --> 00:45:13.143 align:middle line:90%
Did you have a question?

00:45:13.143 --> 00:45:14.810 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well, it
was I was essentially

00:45:14.810 --> 00:45:17.270 align:middle line:84%
going to say that it might
have to do with whether or not

00:45:17.270 --> 00:45:19.130 align:middle line:90%
the reversion is temporary.

00:45:19.130 --> 00:45:21.470 align:middle line:84%
If the reversion of the
verb is a permanent thing,

00:45:21.470 --> 00:45:23.887 align:middle line:84%
and you won't be able to restore
it to the previous state,

00:45:23.887 --> 00:45:25.130 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:45:25.130 --> 00:45:29.630 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Ah, so
if you unlock a door,

00:45:29.630 --> 00:45:31.250 align:middle line:90%
you can lock it again.

00:45:31.250 --> 00:45:33.770 align:middle line:84%
Of course, if you
can unsink ships,

00:45:33.770 --> 00:45:35.624 align:middle line:84%
you can probably sink
them again, right?

00:45:35.624 --> 00:45:37.200 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:37.200 --> 00:45:39.200 align:middle line:84%
This is probably going
to get expensive and wet.

00:45:39.200 --> 00:45:42.617 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, so it's presumably
something you can do.

00:45:42.617 --> 00:45:43.117 align:middle line:90%
Raquel?

00:45:43.117 --> 00:45:44.367 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I have two thoughts.

00:45:44.367 --> 00:45:46.910 align:middle line:84%
And the first thought is that
"unlock" is an actual word,

00:45:46.910 --> 00:45:49.110 align:middle line:84%
but I don't think "unsink"
is an actual word.

00:45:49.110 --> 00:45:53.570 align:middle line:84%
So maybe we think of "unlock"
as its own specific word

00:45:53.570 --> 00:45:55.355 align:middle line:90%
that we can add "able" to.

00:45:55.355 --> 00:45:57.930 align:middle line:84%
And then, the second thought
is that "unbearable",

00:45:57.930 --> 00:46:00.760 align:middle line:84%
that breaks this, because
we don't think "unbear."

00:46:00.760 --> 00:46:02.635 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh,
that's a very-- that's--

00:46:02.635 --> 00:46:04.160 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You can't
unbear a bear.

00:46:04.160 --> 00:46:04.302 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:46:04.302 --> 00:46:05.469 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Well, yeah.

00:46:05.469 --> 00:46:07.940 align:middle line:84%
So but OK, those are both
really interesting thoughts.

00:46:07.940 --> 00:46:09.680 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the first
thought is what

00:46:09.680 --> 00:46:12.410 align:middle line:84%
we're arguing about, is whether
"unsink" is a word or not.

00:46:12.410 --> 00:46:15.840 align:middle line:84%
And I guess the answer
that's emerging is, sort of.

00:46:15.840 --> 00:46:17.820 align:middle line:84%
It's just not something
you do all the time.

00:46:17.820 --> 00:46:21.050 align:middle line:84%
And so our intuitions
about it are kind of fuzzy.

00:46:21.050 --> 00:46:25.010 align:middle line:84%
"Unbearable" is a
really nice example.

00:46:25.010 --> 00:46:27.950 align:middle line:84%
Basically, what that's
telling us is that--

00:46:27.950 --> 00:46:30.340 align:middle line:90%
what the heck is it telling us?

00:46:30.340 --> 00:46:31.340 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It's unbeared?

00:46:31.340 --> 00:46:33.980 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So "bearable,"
right, the adjective

00:46:33.980 --> 00:46:41.270 align:middle line:84%
"bearable," it has a
couple of meanings.

00:46:41.270 --> 00:46:46.640 align:middle line:84%
The meaning that you have in
mind, the meaning that you

00:46:46.640 --> 00:46:49.495 align:middle line:84%
have in mind-- let me remind my
computer not to do that, sorry.

00:46:49.495 --> 00:46:50.870 align:middle line:84%
The meaning that
you have in mind

00:46:50.870 --> 00:46:55.160 align:middle line:84%
is one where "bearable,"
it doesn't just mean

00:46:55.160 --> 00:46:57.260 align:middle line:90%
"can be carried," right?

00:46:57.260 --> 00:47:01.430 align:middle line:84%
It means something more
emotional than that.

00:47:01.430 --> 00:47:06.380 align:middle line:84%
And no, but it can have that
kind of emotional meaning

00:47:06.380 --> 00:47:09.650 align:middle line:90%
when you're bearing-- you know.

00:47:09.650 --> 00:47:11.990 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, what?

00:47:11.990 --> 00:47:18.110 align:middle line:84%
"She bore the loss of
her stock holdings well."

00:47:18.110 --> 00:47:19.850 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, so I don't know.

00:47:19.850 --> 00:47:21.650 align:middle line:90%
So maybe, OK.

00:47:21.650 --> 00:47:24.650 align:middle line:84%
So I guess what we're learning
is that although you cannot

00:47:24.650 --> 00:47:27.050 align:middle line:90%
unbear something--

00:47:27.050 --> 00:47:30.860 align:middle line:84%
maybe because there's no
sense in which you can reverse

00:47:30.860 --> 00:47:33.560 align:middle line:90%
the bearing of something--

00:47:33.560 --> 00:47:35.177 align:middle line:84%
there is an
adjective "bearable,"

00:47:35.177 --> 00:47:37.260 align:middle line:84%
and you can add the other
"un-" to that, the "un-"

00:47:37.260 --> 00:47:40.100 align:middle line:84%
that attaches to
"bearable," to adjectives,

00:47:40.100 --> 00:47:41.210 align:middle line:90%
that means "not bearable."

00:47:41.210 --> 00:47:41.840 align:middle line:90%
There, yes.

00:47:41.840 --> 00:47:42.980 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

00:47:42.980 --> 00:47:45.380 align:middle line:84%
I got distracted by
emotions, as so often.

00:47:45.380 --> 00:47:50.330 align:middle line:84%
But yeah that, just has to
do with the fact that "un-"--

00:47:50.330 --> 00:47:53.660 align:middle line:84%
we're now debating about
whether you can unsink a ship,

00:47:53.660 --> 00:47:58.155 align:middle line:84%
but you surely
can't unbear a loss.

00:47:58.155 --> 00:47:59.780 align:middle line:84%
I don't even know
what that would mean.

00:47:59.780 --> 00:48:02.750 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah.

00:48:02.750 --> 00:48:03.830 align:middle line:90%
Wow, lots of questions.

00:48:03.830 --> 00:48:05.120 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:48:05.120 --> 00:48:07.940 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think it's about
the number of times [INAUDIBLE]..

00:48:07.940 --> 00:48:11.930 align:middle line:84%
Like we used to say "follow,"
and now we can say "unfollow."

00:48:11.930 --> 00:48:13.820 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, yeah.

00:48:13.820 --> 00:48:15.362 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, yeah, "follow"
and "unfollow"

00:48:15.362 --> 00:48:16.820 align:middle line:84%
is a really good
example, actually.

00:48:16.820 --> 00:48:20.300 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so it used to not be
possible to unfollow things.

00:48:20.300 --> 00:48:23.000 align:middle line:84%
One crucial
difference, I guess, is

00:48:23.000 --> 00:48:26.600 align:middle line:84%
that if you wrap something
or tie something,

00:48:26.600 --> 00:48:29.357 align:middle line:84%
you change it so that
it's different now

00:48:29.357 --> 00:48:30.690 align:middle line:90%
as a result of what you've done.

00:48:30.690 --> 00:48:33.177 align:middle line:84%
So if you tie something, it
used to be just a string,

00:48:33.177 --> 00:48:34.010 align:middle line:90%
and now it's a knot.

00:48:34.010 --> 00:48:36.650 align:middle line:84%
Or if you wrap something,
it used to be a thing,

00:48:36.650 --> 00:48:38.240 align:middle line:90%
and now it's a present.

00:48:38.240 --> 00:48:41.540 align:middle line:84%
If you sink a ship, this is what
we've all been talking about.

00:48:41.540 --> 00:48:45.080 align:middle line:84%
It goes from being on top of the
water to being underneath it.

00:48:45.080 --> 00:48:51.390 align:middle line:84%
As opposed to "follow," if you
follow someone in real life,

00:48:51.390 --> 00:48:53.520 align:middle line:90%
you don't do anything to them.

00:48:53.520 --> 00:48:55.230 align:middle line:84%
They're still the
same as they were.

00:48:55.230 --> 00:48:56.780 align:middle line:90%
We kind of have this intuition.

00:48:56.780 --> 00:48:59.000 align:middle line:84%
The "follow" that you
can unfollow someone

00:48:59.000 --> 00:49:01.340 align:middle line:84%
is something that changes
them from a person who

00:49:01.340 --> 00:49:04.640 align:middle line:84%
has n number of followers
to a person who has

00:49:04.640 --> 00:49:06.570 align:middle line:90%
n plus 1 number of followers.

00:49:06.570 --> 00:49:09.230 align:middle line:84%
So there's something about
whether the verb changes

00:49:09.230 --> 00:49:11.120 align:middle line:90%
the state of the object.

00:49:11.120 --> 00:49:14.180 align:middle line:84%
I think that's got something
to do with all this, which

00:49:14.180 --> 00:49:17.420 align:middle line:84%
is why these examples about
unthinking ships and breaking

00:49:17.420 --> 00:49:21.080 align:middle line:84%
pots are kind of
interesting to think about.

00:49:21.080 --> 00:49:21.800 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:49:21.800 --> 00:49:24.170 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It turns
out I just searched

00:49:24.170 --> 00:49:26.660 align:middle line:84%
[INAUDIBLE] "unsink" on
the Scrabble dictionary,

00:49:26.660 --> 00:49:27.800 align:middle line:90%
and it says no.

00:49:27.800 --> 00:49:30.940 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Well, Scrabble
thinks it's not a word.

00:49:30.940 --> 00:49:33.140 align:middle line:84%
So I mean, Scrabble,
they have to have rules.

00:49:33.140 --> 00:49:35.660 align:middle line:84%
They have to draw a
line somewhere, yeah.

00:49:35.660 --> 00:49:36.380 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:49:36.380 --> 00:49:41.930 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
they make [INAUDIBLE]..

00:49:41.930 --> 00:49:45.890 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So Scrabble, we
should have a Scrabble version

00:49:45.890 --> 00:49:48.710 align:middle line:84%
of 24.900 where we try to figure
out what's going on together.

00:49:48.710 --> 00:49:50.690 align:middle line:84%
But Scrabble is
not English, right?

00:49:50.690 --> 00:49:53.760 align:middle line:84%
It's something related to
English, but not the same.

00:49:53.760 --> 00:49:55.430 align:middle line:84%
We're allowed to have
our own intuitions

00:49:55.430 --> 00:49:58.005 align:middle line:84%
about what things are words
and what things aren't, Yeah?

00:49:58.005 --> 00:50:00.380 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I'm not sure
how to phrase this question,

00:50:00.380 --> 00:50:02.150 align:middle line:84%
but it's like you
started off the lecture

00:50:02.150 --> 00:50:04.425 align:middle line:84%
by saying that meaning
comes from morphology.

00:50:04.425 --> 00:50:05.300 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

00:50:05.300 --> 00:50:07.092 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And now we're
trying to, I guess,

00:50:07.092 --> 00:50:09.000 align:middle line:84%
figure out what
[INAUDIBLE] means, right?

00:50:09.000 --> 00:50:11.250 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So one way
to think about it would be

00:50:11.250 --> 00:50:14.000 align:middle line:84%
to think, so this stuff about
which verbs can you attach

00:50:14.000 --> 00:50:16.470 align:middle line:90%
"un-" to, where I just said--

00:50:16.470 --> 00:50:19.550 align:middle line:84%
well, so we started by saying
"un-" can attach to verbs.

00:50:19.550 --> 00:50:23.120 align:middle line:84%
And it gives you a
new verb that means,

00:50:23.120 --> 00:50:26.720 align:middle line:84%
"take the thing that is
in the state resulting

00:50:26.720 --> 00:50:30.050 align:middle line:84%
from the action of the verb
and put it back in the state

00:50:30.050 --> 00:50:32.750 align:middle line:84%
that it was in before
you did the verb."

00:50:32.750 --> 00:50:36.740 align:middle line:84%
That's one way of defining
what "un-" means, yeah?

00:50:36.740 --> 00:50:39.330 align:middle line:90%
And then, did that make sense?

00:50:39.330 --> 00:50:42.020 align:middle line:84%
So if you untie something,
what does that do?

00:50:42.020 --> 00:50:44.780 align:middle line:84%
It takes something which is
tied, which is in the state

00:50:44.780 --> 00:50:47.180 align:middle line:84%
that you're in as a result
of the verb "to tie,"

00:50:47.180 --> 00:50:51.240 align:middle line:84%
and converts it into something
which is not tied anymore.

00:50:51.240 --> 00:50:54.290 align:middle line:84%
That's what it is to
"untie" something.

00:50:54.290 --> 00:50:57.080 align:middle line:84%
So if that's what
"un-" means, then yes,

00:50:57.080 --> 00:50:58.923 align:middle line:84%
you should be able to
attach it to verbs.

00:50:58.923 --> 00:51:00.590 align:middle line:84%
But you should only
be able to attach it

00:51:00.590 --> 00:51:03.230 align:middle line:84%
to verbs that change the
state of their objects

00:51:03.230 --> 00:51:06.620 align:middle line:84%
because it makes
reference to this change

00:51:06.620 --> 00:51:08.880 align:middle line:90%
of the state of the object.

00:51:08.880 --> 00:51:11.960 align:middle line:84%
This is me making stuff up right
here in front of all of you,

00:51:11.960 --> 00:51:13.670 align:middle line:90%
but this seems reasonable to me.

00:51:13.670 --> 00:51:16.220 align:middle line:84%
I think it might be
something like that.

00:51:16.220 --> 00:51:16.820 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:51:16.820 --> 00:51:19.535 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Wouldn't that
make "unthink" valid?

00:51:19.535 --> 00:51:20.660 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: It should.

00:51:20.660 --> 00:51:22.570 align:middle line:84%
Well, so it should
be valid, yes.

00:51:22.570 --> 00:51:23.360 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: When you
think something,

00:51:23.360 --> 00:51:24.527 align:middle line:90%
doesn't it change the state?

00:51:24.527 --> 00:51:27.320 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: It certainly
does change the state, yes.

00:51:27.320 --> 00:51:28.500 align:middle line:90%
Gosh, is that the time?

00:51:28.500 --> 00:51:29.000 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:51:29.000 --> 00:51:30.980 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:51:30.980 --> 00:51:35.060 align:middle line:84%
So look, this is-- so for me,
at least, this is what is cool

00:51:35.060 --> 00:51:36.950 align:middle line:90%
and fun about linguistics.

00:51:36.950 --> 00:51:39.790 align:middle line:84%
Here it is, day 3, and
we are right on the edge.

00:51:39.790 --> 00:51:42.290 align:middle line:84%
I'm a professional linguist,
this is what I do for a living,

00:51:42.290 --> 00:51:45.530 align:middle line:84%
and I'm not quite sure what
the answer to this question is.

00:51:45.530 --> 00:51:48.950 align:middle line:84%
So you guys could be
the ones who figure out

00:51:48.950 --> 00:51:51.200 align:middle line:84%
what the heck is going
on with "unsink,"

00:51:51.200 --> 00:51:53.210 align:middle line:84%
to which the answer
could be, yeah,

00:51:53.210 --> 00:51:55.040 align:middle line:90%
"unsink" is more or less OK.

00:51:55.040 --> 00:51:57.260 align:middle line:84%
It's just not something
people do all the time,

00:51:57.260 --> 00:51:58.760 align:middle line:84%
and so we're not
used to hearing it.

00:51:58.760 --> 00:52:01.370 align:middle line:84%
Maybe that's the easy way of
getting out of where I am.

00:52:01.370 --> 00:52:03.950 align:middle line:84%
Or maybe there's more to
say about what exactly "un-"

00:52:03.950 --> 00:52:05.510 align:middle line:90%
does to the meaning of the verb.

00:52:05.510 --> 00:52:07.520 align:middle line:84%
But that's the kind of
answer to your question

00:52:07.520 --> 00:52:08.540 align:middle line:90%
that we would get--

00:52:08.540 --> 00:52:11.730 align:middle line:84%
that if we define everything
about the meaning of "un-"

00:52:11.730 --> 00:52:14.450 align:middle line:84%
that that will help us
understand why it can attach

00:52:14.450 --> 00:52:16.280 align:middle line:84%
to some verbs but
not others, yeah.

00:52:16.280 --> 00:52:19.290 align:middle line:84%
And as you can see, we're
not quite there, maybe.

00:52:19.290 --> 00:52:21.740 align:middle line:90%
Joseph?

00:52:21.740 --> 00:52:26.000 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So well, I guess
if you put it together--

00:52:26.000 --> 00:52:28.740 align:middle line:84%
if you have the verb "help,"
could you help somebody--

00:52:28.740 --> 00:52:29.615 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes?

00:52:29.615 --> 00:52:31.262 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:52:31.262 --> 00:52:33.095 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Well,
you can be unhelpful.

00:52:33.095 --> 00:52:33.770 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Unhelpful.

00:52:33.770 --> 00:52:34.812 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, god.

00:52:34.812 --> 00:52:37.117 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:52:37.117 --> 00:52:39.200 align:middle line:84%
But there, you're adding
it to an adjective, yeah.

00:52:39.200 --> 00:52:42.842 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, yeah, so you
cannot unhelp someone.

00:52:42.842 --> 00:52:44.570 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:52:44.570 --> 00:52:47.960 align:middle line:84%
So I mean this is a good example
of something somebody should

00:52:47.960 --> 00:52:49.700 align:middle line:84%
have said to me when
I first said, well,

00:52:49.700 --> 00:52:54.110 align:middle line:84%
some verbs change the state of
the object, and others don't.

00:52:54.110 --> 00:52:57.320 align:middle line:84%
So if I burn something,
it goes from being a thing

00:52:57.320 --> 00:52:58.830 align:middle line:90%
to being a pile of carbon.

00:52:58.830 --> 00:53:00.980 align:middle line:84%
And if I break
something, it goes

00:53:00.980 --> 00:53:02.270 align:middle line:90%
from being a thing to being--

00:53:02.270 --> 00:53:03.770 align:middle line:84%
if I sink a ship,
it goes from being

00:53:03.770 --> 00:53:05.645 align:middle line:84%
on top of the water to
being under the water.

00:53:05.645 --> 00:53:09.170 align:middle line:84%
If I help someone,
maybe they're the same

00:53:09.170 --> 00:53:12.922 align:middle line:84%
as they were before, just
in a better position?

00:53:12.922 --> 00:53:15.380 align:middle line:84%
This is the place where you're
entitled to ask, what do you

00:53:15.380 --> 00:53:16.700 align:middle line:90%
mean by changing the state?

00:53:16.700 --> 00:53:18.110 align:middle line:90%
What counts as the state?

00:53:18.110 --> 00:53:20.180 align:middle line:90%
And we really must go on.

00:53:20.180 --> 00:53:20.810 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:53:20.810 --> 00:53:22.925 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
reversible change in state?

00:53:22.925 --> 00:53:24.800 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I guess
yeah, maybe that's--

00:53:24.800 --> 00:53:27.020 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So you can't
exactly revert your help.

00:53:27.020 --> 00:53:28.310 align:middle line:90%
Like you can hurt someone.

00:53:28.310 --> 00:53:28.700 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:53:28.700 --> 00:53:29.420 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Following
that, but you

00:53:29.420 --> 00:53:31.590 align:middle line:84%
can't exactly undo the help
that you've already done.

00:53:31.590 --> 00:53:32.270 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I don't know.

00:53:32.270 --> 00:53:34.353 align:middle line:84%
I mean, look, suppose I
write you a recommendation

00:53:34.353 --> 00:53:35.540 align:middle line:90%
letter for a job, right?

00:53:35.540 --> 00:53:36.680 align:middle line:90%
That's helpful to you.

00:53:36.680 --> 00:53:37.340 align:middle line:90%
I helped him.

00:53:37.340 --> 00:53:39.380 align:middle line:84%
And then, suppose I write
another letter saying,

00:53:39.380 --> 00:53:39.963 align:middle line:90%
you know what?

00:53:39.963 --> 00:53:41.180 align:middle line:90%
I take it all back.

00:53:41.180 --> 00:53:43.670 align:middle line:84%
In fact, it's the opposite
of everything I just said.

00:53:43.670 --> 00:53:46.350 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
person [INAUDIBLE]..

00:53:46.350 --> 00:53:47.488 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:53:47.488 --> 00:53:48.780 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Hold on, guys.

00:53:48.780 --> 00:53:51.800 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, yeah, yeah, no,
I see what you mean.

00:53:51.800 --> 00:53:55.397 align:middle line:84%
All right, so moving on,
then, from this slide,

00:53:55.397 --> 00:53:57.230 align:middle line:84%
which has generated so
much cool discussion.

00:53:57.230 --> 00:53:59.180 align:middle line:90%
This is great.

00:53:59.180 --> 00:54:00.050 align:middle line:90%
So trees.

00:54:00.050 --> 00:54:03.440 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, there, why is
"unlockable" ambiguous?

00:54:03.440 --> 00:54:05.960 align:middle line:84%
Because it has two trees--
or to put it another way,

00:54:05.960 --> 00:54:09.020 align:middle line:84%
there are these two morphemes
you're adding to "lock,"

00:54:09.020 --> 00:54:11.450 align:middle line:84%
and you can add them
in either order,

00:54:11.450 --> 00:54:14.570 align:middle line:84%
with the result that the
word is ambiguous in the way

00:54:14.570 --> 00:54:18.630 align:middle line:90%
that we suspect, we expect.

00:54:18.630 --> 00:54:22.830 align:middle line:84%
So here's a model for how
we're going to make words.

00:54:22.830 --> 00:54:26.270 align:middle line:84%
We're going to take
morphemes, pairs of morphemes,

00:54:26.270 --> 00:54:28.700 align:middle line:84%
and we're going to
glom them together.

00:54:28.700 --> 00:54:31.893 align:middle line:84%
And we feel silly saying the
word "glom" over and over

00:54:31.893 --> 00:54:33.810 align:middle line:84%
again, so we've made up
another word for that.

00:54:33.810 --> 00:54:35.360 align:middle line:90%
We call it "merge."

00:54:35.360 --> 00:54:40.100 align:middle line:84%
So "merge" is this process that
allows you to take two things

00:54:40.100 --> 00:54:43.700 align:middle line:84%
and put them together
and make a new thing.

00:54:43.700 --> 00:54:47.780 align:middle line:84%
So you take "un-" and "lock,"
and you merge them together

00:54:47.780 --> 00:54:51.020 align:middle line:90%
to make "unlock."

00:54:51.020 --> 00:54:55.150 align:middle line:84%
Merge is recursive,
which means only that it

00:54:55.150 --> 00:54:57.150 align:middle line:90%
can reapply to its own output.

00:54:57.150 --> 00:55:01.550 align:middle line:84%
So you take "un-" and "lock"
and merge them to make "unlock,"

00:55:01.550 --> 00:55:03.740 align:middle line:84%
and now you have a
new thing, "unlock."

00:55:03.740 --> 00:55:06.170 align:middle line:90%
And that can also undergo merge.

00:55:06.170 --> 00:55:09.110 align:middle line:84%
So you can take "unlock"
and merge it with "able"

00:55:09.110 --> 00:55:10.550 align:middle line:90%
to get you "unlockable".

00:55:10.550 --> 00:55:13.580 align:middle line:84%
That's what's going on
in the tree on the left.

00:55:13.580 --> 00:55:18.440 align:middle line:84%
That's all linguists mean when
we say that merge is recursive.

00:55:18.440 --> 00:55:20.300 align:middle line:84%
You may sometimes
hear people have

00:55:20.300 --> 00:55:21.660 align:middle line:90%
arguments about what it means.

00:55:21.660 --> 00:55:22.560 align:middle line:90%
That's what it means.

00:55:22.560 --> 00:55:25.310 align:middle line:84%
So it means once you've
merged two things to create

00:55:25.310 --> 00:55:27.200 align:middle line:84%
a new thing, you can
take that new thing

00:55:27.200 --> 00:55:30.050 align:middle line:84%
and apply merge to it as well
and make new things that way.

00:55:30.050 --> 00:55:32.250 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's all it means.

00:55:32.250 --> 00:55:36.240 align:middle line:84%
OK, and then there are
statements which we have to put

00:55:36.240 --> 00:55:41.220 align:middle line:84%
somewhere, like "-able"
needs to merge with a verb

00:55:41.220 --> 00:55:45.390 align:middle line:90%
and the result is an adjective--

00:55:45.390 --> 00:55:48.540 align:middle line:90%
things like that.

00:55:48.540 --> 00:55:49.535 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:55:49.535 --> 00:55:51.285 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Does the
tree have to be binary?

00:55:51.285 --> 00:55:54.180 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Ah, so I said
we're going to take two things,

00:55:54.180 --> 00:55:56.305 align:middle line:84%
and we're going to merge
them and make a new thing.

00:55:56.305 --> 00:55:58.360 align:middle line:90%


00:55:58.360 --> 00:56:01.440 align:middle line:84%
A really good question about
which people have arguments,

00:56:01.440 --> 00:56:03.330 align:middle line:90%
yeah?

00:56:03.330 --> 00:56:07.930 align:middle line:84%
Actually, it seems to be the
case that the trees are binary,

00:56:07.930 --> 00:56:08.980 align:middle line:90%
which is interesting.

00:56:08.980 --> 00:56:11.370 align:middle line:84%
So to put it another
way, there are

00:56:11.370 --> 00:56:13.110 align:middle line:84%
lots of places
where you can't tell

00:56:13.110 --> 00:56:16.390 align:middle line:84%
whether the tree is binary,
or ternary, or whatever.

00:56:16.390 --> 00:56:18.330 align:middle line:84%
But in every place
where you can tell,

00:56:18.330 --> 00:56:20.520 align:middle line:84%
nobody has come up with
a convincing example

00:56:20.520 --> 00:56:23.520 align:middle line:84%
of a tree that is anything other
than binary, which is itself

00:56:23.520 --> 00:56:24.450 align:middle line:90%
kind of interesting.

00:56:24.450 --> 00:56:27.820 align:middle line:84%
I want to try to
understand why that is.

00:56:27.820 --> 00:56:30.220 align:middle line:84%
We're going to be doing lots
of merging in morphology.

00:56:30.220 --> 00:56:31.987 align:middle line:84%
And then, not so
much in phonology.

00:56:31.987 --> 00:56:33.570 align:middle line:84%
And then, when we
get to syntax, there

00:56:33.570 --> 00:56:36.970 align:middle line:84%
will be much merging, many
merging of things-- words,

00:56:36.970 --> 00:56:40.240 align:middle line:84%
putting them together to make
phrases of various kinds.

00:56:40.240 --> 00:56:44.730 align:middle line:84%
So try to get comfortable
with the concept of merge.

00:56:44.730 --> 00:56:46.180 align:middle line:90%
It should be easy.

00:56:46.180 --> 00:56:49.500 align:middle line:90%
It's not a hard concept.

00:56:49.500 --> 00:56:53.480 align:middle line:90%
OK, this is great.

00:56:53.480 --> 00:56:54.980 align:middle line:84%
I just want to
talk-- ooh, I'm going

00:56:54.980 --> 00:56:56.960 align:middle line:84%
to talk a little
more systematically

00:56:56.960 --> 00:56:58.010 align:middle line:90%
about allomorphs.

00:56:58.010 --> 00:57:02.420 align:middle line:84%
So I mentioned before,
you take morphemes,

00:57:02.420 --> 00:57:05.330 align:middle line:84%
you merge them together,
you create new things,

00:57:05.330 --> 00:57:08.030 align:middle line:84%
and what often happens is
that one or another or both

00:57:08.030 --> 00:57:10.010 align:middle line:84%
of the morphemes
will change its form.

00:57:10.010 --> 00:57:11.877 align:middle line:84%
So the past tense of
"leap" is "leaped"

00:57:11.877 --> 00:57:12.530 align:middle line:90%
[pronounced like "lept"].

00:57:12.530 --> 00:57:15.170 align:middle line:84%
The verb changes from "leap" to
"leap" [pronounced like "lep"].

00:57:15.170 --> 00:57:17.450 align:middle line:84%
The past tense suffix can
either be "t," or "d,"

00:57:17.450 --> 00:57:19.890 align:middle line:90%
or some other things.

00:57:19.890 --> 00:57:22.250 align:middle line:84%
So I want to talk a
little bit systematically

00:57:22.250 --> 00:57:24.620 align:middle line:90%
about these allomorphs.

00:57:24.620 --> 00:57:28.970 align:middle line:84%
I'll start by saying that there
are cases where there just

00:57:28.970 --> 00:57:33.440 align:middle line:84%
isn't anything interesting,
there isn't anything helpful

00:57:33.440 --> 00:57:34.640 align:middle line:90%
to say.

00:57:34.640 --> 00:57:38.090 align:middle line:84%
Past tense of "go" is
"went," and it just is.

00:57:38.090 --> 00:57:40.670 align:middle line:84%
If you want to learn English,
you've got to learn that.

00:57:40.670 --> 00:57:42.567 align:middle line:90%
There's nothing else to say.

00:57:42.567 --> 00:57:44.150 align:middle line:84%
But there are other
places where there

00:57:44.150 --> 00:57:47.585 align:middle line:84%
are general laws
about which allomorph

00:57:47.585 --> 00:57:49.460 align:middle line:84%
you're going to get
under which circumstance.

00:57:49.460 --> 00:57:51.260 align:middle line:84%
We alluded to that
a little bit when

00:57:51.260 --> 00:57:55.100 align:middle line:84%
we were talking about cats
and dogs, that "s" and "z."

00:57:55.100 --> 00:57:57.170 align:middle line:84%
Those allomorphs
of the plural are

00:57:57.170 --> 00:57:59.250 align:middle line:84%
conditioned by the sound
that's before them.

00:57:59.250 --> 00:58:00.960 align:middle line:90%
And we'll talk more about that.

00:58:00.960 --> 00:58:03.410 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to give you an
example where there's more

00:58:03.410 --> 00:58:05.490 align:middle line:90%
to say about the allomorphs.

00:58:05.490 --> 00:58:09.050 align:middle line:90%
This is an example from Polish.

00:58:09.050 --> 00:58:13.010 align:middle line:84%
I should say I've spelled
Polish here in a strange way.

00:58:13.010 --> 00:58:15.230 align:middle line:84%
Anybody here speaks
Polish, this isn't

00:58:15.230 --> 00:58:17.360 align:middle line:84%
the way you're used to
seeing Polish spelled.

00:58:17.360 --> 00:58:20.650 align:middle line:84%
So I've spelled it in a way that
makes it easier for people who

00:58:20.650 --> 00:58:23.150 align:middle line:84%
don't speak Polish to see what
the words-- how the words are

00:58:23.150 --> 00:58:25.320 align:middle line:90%
pronounced, more or less.

00:58:25.320 --> 00:58:27.480 align:middle line:90%
So here are some Polish words.

00:58:27.480 --> 00:58:30.590 align:middle line:84%
They mean "language,"
"pot," "juice," and "bow,"

00:58:30.590 --> 00:58:33.210 align:middle line:84%
and you've got
some plurals there.

00:58:33.210 --> 00:58:35.690 align:middle line:84%
So does anybody
here speak Polish?

00:58:35.690 --> 00:58:38.550 align:middle line:84%
Excellent, I can just make
anything up that I want.

00:58:38.550 --> 00:58:39.050 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:58:39.050 --> 00:58:41.000 align:middle line:84%
Believe me, this is
how Polish works.

00:58:41.000 --> 00:58:41.720 align:middle line:90%
It is, really.

00:58:41.720 --> 00:58:43.580 align:middle line:90%
I'm not making things up.

00:58:43.580 --> 00:58:45.247 align:middle line:84%
Does anybody find any
morphemes in here?

00:58:45.247 --> 00:58:47.497 align:middle line:84%
I mean, there's morphemes
that mean "language", "pot,"

00:58:47.497 --> 00:58:48.320 align:middle line:90%
"juice," and "bow."

00:58:48.320 --> 00:58:50.660 align:middle line:90%
What's the plural morpheme?

00:58:50.660 --> 00:58:51.350 align:middle line:90%
"Ee," yeah?

00:58:51.350 --> 00:58:53.300 align:middle line:84%
So it's a suffix, it's
spelled with the letter

00:58:53.300 --> 00:58:55.250 align:middle line:90%
"I" it's pronounced, "ee".

00:58:55.250 --> 00:58:58.130 align:middle line:90%
No allomorphy here so far.

00:58:58.130 --> 00:58:59.750 align:middle line:90%
Here are some more words--

00:58:59.750 --> 00:59:04.790 align:middle line:84%
"bank of a river," "debt,"
and "lie," which have plurals.

00:59:04.790 --> 00:59:06.950 align:middle line:84%
The idea of a plural of
"lie" is a little strange,

00:59:06.950 --> 00:59:09.530 align:middle line:84%
if you ask me, but
nobody asked me.

00:59:09.530 --> 00:59:12.290 align:middle line:84%
If you had the plural morpheme,
plural morpheme there is "ee",

00:59:12.290 --> 00:59:14.520 align:middle line:90%
and you get those plurals.

00:59:14.520 --> 00:59:16.407 align:middle line:90%
Anybody seeing any allomorphs?

00:59:16.407 --> 00:59:19.030 align:middle line:90%


00:59:19.030 --> 00:59:21.730 align:middle line:84%
So the plural suffix
is still the same,

00:59:21.730 --> 00:59:25.780 align:middle line:84%
but "debt," for example,
has two allomorphs.

00:59:25.780 --> 00:59:27.790 align:middle line:90%
What are they?

00:59:27.790 --> 00:59:29.290 align:middle line:84%
Somebody attempt
to pronounce them.

00:59:29.290 --> 00:59:32.330 align:middle line:90%


00:59:32.330 --> 00:59:35.390 align:middle line:84%
It's OK, none of
us speak Polish.

00:59:35.390 --> 00:59:35.900 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:59:35.900 --> 00:59:37.010 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Duk" and "dug?"

00:59:37.010 --> 00:59:39.950 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, we've got
"duk" and "dug", yeah.

00:59:39.950 --> 00:59:42.050 align:middle line:84%
So "debt" has one
allomorph that ends

00:59:42.050 --> 00:59:44.645 align:middle line:84%
in a "k" and another
allomorph that ends in a "g."

00:59:44.645 --> 00:59:47.800 align:middle line:90%


00:59:47.800 --> 00:59:49.840 align:middle line:84%
What's the rule,
do you think, that

00:59:49.840 --> 00:59:51.895 align:middle line:84%
determines when you get
"k" and when you get "g"?

00:59:51.895 --> 00:59:57.660 align:middle line:90%


00:59:57.660 --> 01:00:00.210 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:00:00.210 --> 01:00:02.970 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Would it have to
do with the content that

01:00:02.970 --> 01:00:09.900 align:middle line:84%
comes before the "l," where
it [INAUDIBLE] like sound like

01:00:09.900 --> 01:00:13.565 align:middle line:84%
[INAUDIBLE],, so
it's like "ch," "k"?

01:00:13.565 --> 01:00:14.690 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, I see.

01:00:14.690 --> 01:00:16.160 align:middle line:90%
That's a neat idea.

01:00:16.160 --> 01:00:18.160 align:middle line:84%
So maybe we could work
out a way-- so all of you

01:00:18.160 --> 01:00:19.960 align:middle line:90%
are realizing the problem.

01:00:19.960 --> 01:00:22.240 align:middle line:84%
The problem is there are
the words we started with,

01:00:22.240 --> 01:00:24.340 align:middle line:84%
where we have all these
words that end in "k,"

01:00:24.340 --> 01:00:25.882 align:middle line:84%
and when you make
the plural, there's

01:00:25.882 --> 01:00:27.170 align:middle line:90%
still a "k" before the "ee".

01:00:27.170 --> 01:00:28.670 align:middle line:84%
And then, we have
these other words,

01:00:28.670 --> 01:00:31.253 align:middle line:84%
where the word ends in "k," and
then, when you add the plural,

01:00:31.253 --> 01:00:31.960 align:middle line:90%
you get a "g."

01:00:31.960 --> 01:00:32.460 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:00:32.460 --> 01:00:34.418 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I feel like we
need more information,

01:00:34.418 --> 01:00:36.880 align:middle line:84%
because even if you look at
the words for "bow" and "lye,"

01:00:36.880 --> 01:00:38.785 align:middle line:84%
they're the same word
in the singular form.

01:00:38.785 --> 01:00:40.160 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Very nice point.

01:00:40.160 --> 01:00:44.260 align:middle line:84%
So this slide was
carefully constructed

01:00:44.260 --> 01:00:46.240 align:middle line:84%
to have what's called
a minimal pair.

01:00:46.240 --> 01:00:50.560 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, "bow" and "lie"
are both pronounced "wuk"

01:00:50.560 --> 01:00:56.110 align:middle line:84%
in the singular, but in
plural, the plural of is "wuki"

01:00:56.110 --> 01:00:59.770 align:middle line:84%
and the plural of
lie is "wugi," yeah?

01:00:59.770 --> 01:01:01.550 align:middle line:84%
This is meant to
make us despair--

01:01:01.550 --> 01:01:04.240 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:01:04.240 --> 01:01:06.790 align:middle line:84%
--about the option that
you were raising right,

01:01:06.790 --> 01:01:10.570 align:middle line:84%
which was maybe if we look
at everything about the word,

01:01:10.570 --> 01:01:13.380 align:middle line:84%
we'll be able to predict which
"k"s become "g"s and which "k"s

01:01:13.380 --> 01:01:14.410 align:middle line:90%
stay "k"s.

01:01:14.410 --> 01:01:16.090 align:middle line:90%
No, give up.

01:01:16.090 --> 01:01:17.830 align:middle line:90%
Despair, yeah?

01:01:17.830 --> 01:01:18.610 align:middle line:90%
Cannot be done.

01:01:18.610 --> 01:01:20.550 align:middle line:84%
We cannot, if we
start with a "k,"

01:01:20.550 --> 01:01:23.050 align:middle line:84%
we cannot predict which "k"s
will become "g"s and which ones

01:01:23.050 --> 01:01:24.230 align:middle line:90%
will not.

01:01:24.230 --> 01:01:24.898 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:01:24.898 --> 01:01:27.190 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] one of
those examples [INAUDIBLE]

01:01:27.190 --> 01:01:30.377 align:middle line:84%
not [INAUDIBLE] things
[INAUDIBLE],, but [INAUDIBLE]..

01:01:30.377 --> 01:01:32.210 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I think
that's a neat idea.

01:01:32.210 --> 01:01:34.810 align:middle line:90%
So can you say more about that?

01:01:34.810 --> 01:01:39.718 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I mean, my first guess
might just be that if the end

01:01:39.718 --> 01:01:42.820 align:middle line:84%
in "g-i", is it
[INAUDIBLE] into a "k"?

01:01:42.820 --> 01:01:45.000 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: OK,
yeah so I'll just

01:01:45.000 --> 01:01:46.750 align:middle line:84%
say again what you
just said, just to make

01:01:46.750 --> 01:01:47.980 align:middle line:90%
sure everybody heard it.

01:01:47.980 --> 01:01:52.420 align:middle line:84%
I framed the problem as,
which "k"s change into "g"s,

01:01:52.420 --> 01:01:54.430 align:middle line:84%
and which "k"s don't
change into "g"s?

01:01:54.430 --> 01:01:57.250 align:middle line:84%
And then we convinced
ourselves, thanks to your point,

01:01:57.250 --> 01:01:59.200 align:middle line:90%
that problem is impossible.

01:01:59.200 --> 01:02:01.990 align:middle line:84%
We will just have to list,
well, sometimes "k" becomes "g."

01:02:01.990 --> 01:02:03.400 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes "k"
doesn't become "g."

01:02:03.400 --> 01:02:07.300 align:middle line:84%
But that's because I phrased
the problem as, which "k"

01:02:07.300 --> 01:02:09.070 align:middle line:84%
becomes "k" and
which one doesn't?

01:02:09.070 --> 01:02:11.697 align:middle line:84%
If we do, if we
look at this problem

01:02:11.697 --> 01:02:13.780 align:middle line:84%
in the other direction--
this is your suggestion--

01:02:13.780 --> 01:02:17.080 align:middle line:84%
if we start with the plural
and think about the singular,

01:02:17.080 --> 01:02:18.790 align:middle line:84%
then we can say to
ourselves, OK, there

01:02:18.790 --> 01:02:22.990 align:middle line:84%
are some nouns that end in "k"
and some nouns that end in "g."

01:02:22.990 --> 01:02:24.850 align:middle line:84%
In the singular,
what's the rule?

01:02:24.850 --> 01:02:27.428 align:middle line:84%
In the singular, do you
get "k," or do you get "g"?

01:02:27.428 --> 01:02:27.970 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "k"

01:02:27.970 --> 01:02:29.590 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "k," always.

01:02:29.590 --> 01:02:35.380 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so what we need for Polish
is the willingness to say,

01:02:35.380 --> 01:02:37.420 align:middle line:84%
yeah, there are
nouns that end in "k"

01:02:37.420 --> 01:02:39.060 align:middle line:90%
and nouns that end in "g."

01:02:39.060 --> 01:02:43.180 align:middle line:84%
The plural suffix is this
letter "i," this "ee."

01:02:43.180 --> 01:02:45.140 align:middle line:90%
And there's a very general rule.

01:02:45.140 --> 01:02:50.310 align:middle line:84%
If you have a "g" at the end
of a word, turn it into a "k."

01:02:50.310 --> 01:02:56.700 align:middle line:84%
So if we're making the
lexicon for Polish,

01:02:56.700 --> 01:02:59.820 align:middle line:84%
what we want is for
the word for "bow"

01:02:59.820 --> 01:03:03.810 align:middle line:84%
to be in the lexicon "wuk,"
and for the word for "lye"

01:03:03.810 --> 01:03:06.060 align:middle line:90%
to be in the lexicon "wug."

01:03:06.060 --> 01:03:09.990 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, and then, the
plural suffix is "ee,"

01:03:09.990 --> 01:03:16.920 align:middle line:84%
and there's a general rule,
"g" at the end of a word

01:03:16.920 --> 01:03:23.190 align:middle line:90%
becomes "k," right?

01:03:23.190 --> 01:03:26.880 align:middle line:84%
This means the lexicon
is a little bit abstract.

01:03:26.880 --> 01:03:30.270 align:middle line:84%
if you ask a Polish speaker,
what's the word for "lye,"

01:03:30.270 --> 01:03:31.950 align:middle line:90%
they're going to say "wuok."

01:03:31.950 --> 01:03:33.480 align:middle line:84%
And if you say,
no, it's not, it's

01:03:33.480 --> 01:03:35.190 align:middle line:84%
"wug," they're going
to be like, who's

01:03:35.190 --> 01:03:37.200 align:middle line:84%
the Polish speaker
around here, you or me?

01:03:37.200 --> 01:03:37.950 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:03:37.950 --> 01:03:40.410 align:middle line:84%
But in fact, you are right and
the Polish speaker is wrong.

01:03:40.410 --> 01:03:42.660 align:middle line:84%
You have the word
for "lye" is "wug,"

01:03:42.660 --> 01:03:44.430 align:middle line:90%
and then there's this rule--

01:03:44.430 --> 01:03:46.930 align:middle line:84%
"g" becomes "k" at
the ends of words.

01:03:46.930 --> 01:03:49.440 align:middle line:84%
What that means is that
the morpheme for "lye,"

01:03:49.440 --> 01:03:52.500 align:middle line:84%
if we want to know, what's
the morpheme for "lye," well,

01:03:52.500 --> 01:03:56.190 align:middle line:84%
it isn't what the speaker
would say it is necessarily.

01:03:56.190 --> 01:03:59.520 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't have to be a morpheme
that you ever hear by itself.

01:03:59.520 --> 01:04:02.470 align:middle line:84%
It only shows up in the
plural, not in the singular.

01:04:02.470 --> 01:04:04.962 align:middle line:84%
That's the most economical
way of talking about Polish.

01:04:04.962 --> 01:04:06.420 align:middle line:84%
Because the fact
is, I can tell you

01:04:06.420 --> 01:04:09.200 align:middle line:84%
this from my vast
knowledge of Polish,

01:04:09.200 --> 01:04:11.700 align:middle line:84%
you will never, no matter how
many Polish words you look at,

01:04:11.700 --> 01:04:13.410 align:middle line:90%
find one that ends with a "g."

01:04:13.410 --> 01:04:14.910 align:middle line:90%
They never do.

01:04:14.910 --> 01:04:17.160 align:middle line:84%
Plenty that end in "k," but
they never end with a "g."

01:04:17.160 --> 01:04:19.935 align:middle line:84%
So this rule is one
that we can rely on.

01:04:19.935 --> 01:04:20.435 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:04:20.435 --> 01:04:23.030 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Does that make
"wug" a bound morpheme?

01:04:23.030 --> 01:04:25.610 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:04:25.610 --> 01:04:30.140 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Well, this morpheme

01:04:30.140 --> 01:04:33.050 align:middle line:84%
is a free morpheme
because it doesn't

01:04:33.050 --> 01:04:35.750 align:middle line:90%
need to combine with anything.

01:04:35.750 --> 01:04:39.680 align:middle line:84%
It has an allomorph,
"wuk," which

01:04:39.680 --> 01:04:41.780 align:middle line:84%
is the result of this
general sound change,

01:04:41.780 --> 01:04:43.610 align:middle line:84%
that "g" becomes "k"
at the ends of words.

01:04:43.610 --> 01:04:46.160 align:middle line:84%
I guess that's the
easiest way to say it.

01:04:46.160 --> 01:04:48.810 align:middle line:90%
That's a good point, yeah.

01:04:48.810 --> 01:04:50.450 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:04:50.450 --> 01:04:51.920 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:04:51.920 --> 01:04:53.480 align:middle line:90%
So this is a place--

01:04:53.480 --> 01:04:56.330 align:middle line:84%
I told you, go has
the past tense went,

01:04:56.330 --> 01:05:00.650 align:middle line:84%
and give up on coming with
sound up with sound changes that

01:05:00.650 --> 01:05:01.490 align:middle line:90%
will get you that.

01:05:01.490 --> 01:05:06.050 align:middle line:84%
That's just our ancestors
being quite peculiar.

01:05:06.050 --> 01:05:09.020 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, but Polish has
this general rule that

01:05:09.020 --> 01:05:10.640 align:middle line:90%
changes final "g" to "k."

01:05:10.640 --> 01:05:13.040 align:middle line:84%
And so for Polish,
we don't in the end

01:05:13.040 --> 01:05:14.510 align:middle line:90%
need to list allomorphs.

01:05:14.510 --> 01:05:17.030 align:middle line:84%
"Lye" has allomorphs
"wuk" and "wug."

01:05:17.030 --> 01:05:18.650 align:middle line:90%
We can, if we want.

01:05:18.650 --> 01:05:19.430 align:middle line:90%
We need a hobby.

01:05:19.430 --> 01:05:20.850 align:middle line:90%
It'll keep us off the streets.

01:05:20.850 --> 01:05:22.580 align:middle line:84%
But it's OK to just
say, no, there's

01:05:22.580 --> 01:05:24.560 align:middle line:84%
a word for "lye,"
"wug," and then there's

01:05:24.560 --> 01:05:27.140 align:middle line:84%
this general sound change law
that changes the "g" to a "k."

01:05:27.140 --> 01:05:28.073 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:05:28.073 --> 01:05:29.698 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is that
just transliteration,

01:05:29.698 --> 01:05:30.890 align:middle line:90%
or is it the actual "wug"?

01:05:30.890 --> 01:05:33.265 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So this is
not Polish orthography, yeah.

01:05:33.265 --> 01:05:35.905 align:middle line:84%
So this is not an
ordinary Polish spelling.

01:05:35.905 --> 01:05:37.280 align:middle line:84%
I think I started
by saying that.

01:05:37.280 --> 01:05:37.670 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:05:37.670 --> 01:05:39.110 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was
just [INAUDIBLE],,

01:05:39.110 --> 01:05:41.090 align:middle line:84%
I don't know Polish, but I
know Ukrainian and Russian.

01:05:41.090 --> 01:05:42.007 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:05:42.007 --> 01:05:43.635 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: So I [INAUDIBLE].

01:05:43.635 --> 01:05:44.510 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

01:05:44.510 --> 01:05:46.640 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And like
I was just checking

01:05:46.640 --> 01:05:47.645 align:middle line:90%
the actual Polish word.

01:05:47.645 --> 01:05:48.520 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

01:05:48.520 --> 01:05:50.020 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And [INAUDIBLE] that
actual Polish word for "wuk,"

01:05:50.020 --> 01:05:51.520 align:middle line:90%
"wohg," actually "g" in there.

01:05:51.520 --> 01:05:53.213 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes, yes.

01:05:53.213 --> 01:05:55.130 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
check the one for "lye"?

01:05:55.130 --> 01:05:55.630 align:middle line:90%
No, no, no--

01:05:55.630 --> 01:05:57.320 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: No,
that's "lye," yeah.

01:05:57.320 --> 01:05:57.920 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:05:57.920 --> 01:05:58.205 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

01:05:58.205 --> 01:05:59.622 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And if
you check the one

01:05:59.622 --> 01:06:00.978 align:middle line:90%
for "lye," that [INAUDIBLE].

01:06:00.978 --> 01:06:01.895 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:06:01.895 --> 01:06:02.770 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:06:02.770 --> 01:06:05.990 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So I think
I started this by saying,

01:06:05.990 --> 01:06:09.020 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to spell these
Polish words in an unusual way.

01:06:09.020 --> 01:06:11.520 align:middle line:84%
And I tried to make it sound
like that was for your benefit,

01:06:11.520 --> 01:06:13.430 align:middle line:84%
so that you would know how
the words were pronounced.

01:06:13.430 --> 01:06:15.163 align:middle line:84%
But I was actually
obscuring the fact--

01:06:15.163 --> 01:06:16.580 align:middle line:84%
which you were
bringing up-- which

01:06:16.580 --> 01:06:20.300 align:middle line:84%
is that Poles spell as though
this rule had not happened.

01:06:20.300 --> 01:06:23.060 align:middle line:84%
So they pronounce the word for
"lye" and the word for "bow"

01:06:23.060 --> 01:06:24.500 align:middle line:90%
the same way.

01:06:24.500 --> 01:06:26.060 align:middle line:84%
But you're right,
they spell the word

01:06:26.060 --> 01:06:27.590 align:middle line:90%
for "lye" ending with the "g."

01:06:27.590 --> 01:06:30.160 align:middle line:84%
They also don't use the letter
W. This is their barred L.

01:06:30.160 --> 01:06:32.217 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yes?

01:06:32.217 --> 01:06:33.800 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So for
language acquisition,

01:06:33.800 --> 01:06:35.240 align:middle line:90%
I've been studying Polish?

01:06:35.240 --> 01:06:39.350 align:middle line:84%
Because it would almost seem
like speakers of Polish gain

01:06:39.350 --> 01:06:42.320 align:middle line:84%
the plural form of the word
before they can be singular.

01:06:42.320 --> 01:06:44.640 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh,
I see what you mean.

01:06:44.640 --> 01:06:46.670 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of work
on the acquisition

01:06:46.670 --> 01:06:49.395 align:middle line:84%
of morphological and
phonological rules like this.

01:06:49.395 --> 01:06:51.020 align:middle line:84%
I don't know, I can't
swear that people

01:06:51.020 --> 01:06:52.270 align:middle line:90%
have worked on this in Polish.

01:06:52.270 --> 01:06:53.910 align:middle line:84%
My understanding
is that children--

01:06:53.910 --> 01:06:55.860 align:middle line:84%
this is a very common
kind of sound change.

01:06:55.860 --> 01:06:58.340 align:middle line:84%
And we'll talk more about
it as the class goes along.

01:06:58.340 --> 01:07:00.860 align:middle line:84%
And my understanding is that
children who are learning

01:07:00.860 --> 01:07:02.840 align:middle line:84%
languages that have this
kind of sound change--

01:07:02.840 --> 01:07:05.210 align:middle line:90%
it's called final devoicing--

01:07:05.210 --> 01:07:07.910 align:middle line:90%
acquire it kind of immediately.

01:07:07.910 --> 01:07:11.890 align:middle line:84%
It's not something that
takes them a while.

01:07:11.890 --> 01:07:13.600 align:middle line:90%
Other questions?

01:07:13.600 --> 01:07:15.210 align:middle line:90%
Children are very smart.

01:07:15.210 --> 01:07:18.040 align:middle line:84%
This is one of the big results
of language acquisition

01:07:18.040 --> 01:07:18.550 align:middle line:90%
research.

01:07:18.550 --> 01:07:22.130 align:middle line:84%
They know things before
you would think they do.

01:07:22.130 --> 01:07:27.050 align:middle line:90%
OK, let's see where we are.

01:07:27.050 --> 01:07:29.377 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's where we are.

01:07:29.377 --> 01:07:31.710 align:middle line:84%
Yep, so we'll never be able
to predict which "k"s change

01:07:31.710 --> 01:07:32.910 align:middle line:90%
to "g" in the plural.

01:07:32.910 --> 01:07:36.480 align:middle line:84%
So what we'll do is posit these
underlying forms over there

01:07:36.480 --> 01:07:39.000 align:middle line:84%
on the right, some ending in
"k," others ending in "g,"

01:07:39.000 --> 01:07:40.890 align:middle line:84%
and then we'll have
a rule, "g" becomes

01:07:40.890 --> 01:07:43.770 align:middle line:90%
"k" at the end of a word.

01:07:43.770 --> 01:07:45.558 align:middle line:90%
And given, the time.

01:07:45.558 --> 01:07:47.100 align:middle line:84%
I think this is a
good place to stop.

01:07:47.100 --> 01:07:48.615 align:middle line:90%
Are there any questions?

01:07:48.615 --> 01:07:49.115 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:07:49.115 --> 01:07:53.730 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] is
there any [INAUDIBLE]??

01:07:53.730 --> 01:07:56.610 align:middle line:90%


01:07:56.610 --> 01:07:57.443 align:middle line:90%
Or like [INAUDIBLE]?

01:07:57.443 --> 01:07:58.735 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: You know what?

01:07:58.735 --> 01:08:00.650 align:middle line:84%
Let's talk about that
when we do phonology

01:08:00.650 --> 01:08:01.650 align:middle line:90%
a little more carefully.

01:08:01.650 --> 01:08:04.770 align:middle line:84%
Because yes, this change from
"g" to "k," you're right,

01:08:04.770 --> 01:08:06.228 align:middle line:84%
it's part of a more
general change.

01:08:06.228 --> 01:08:08.187 align:middle line:84%
There are lots of things
like this that happen.

01:08:08.187 --> 01:08:09.490 align:middle line:90%
And it's a very common change.

01:08:09.490 --> 01:08:11.990 align:middle line:84%
So we'll try to talk more about
it as we get closer to that.

01:08:11.990 --> 01:08:14.140 align:middle line:90%
Good question.

01:08:14.140 --> 01:08:17.550 align:middle line:84%
So again, problem set due
today, interpreted generously.

01:08:17.550 --> 01:08:18.493 align:middle line:90%
It's due by dawn.

01:08:18.493 --> 01:08:20.160 align:middle line:84%
And I'll try to put
up a new problem set

01:08:20.160 --> 01:08:22.400 align:middle line:90%
before today is over.

01:08:22.400 --> 01:08:27.000 align:middle line:90%